WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:09.950
- All right. Well, welcome
everyone for coming out

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:14.950
for our 2022 spring Chubb
Fellowship with Hilary Hahn.

00:00:15.825 --> 00:00:18.992
(audience applauding)

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.830
So, I can't say enough how
much of an honor this is

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:36.830
to be up here introducing
Hilary Hahn to all of you.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.730
It's just been quite the journey

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.800
in terms of getting her here,
and now the day has come.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.780
So it is really, really
thrilling to have three-time

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.110
Grammy Award winning violin
virtuoso, Hilary Hahn with us.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.860
So before we begin today's conversation,

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.510
I ask that you make sure you
turn off all your cell phones

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:58.950
and refrain from taking any photographs,

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.961
except for our official photographer,

00:01:00.961 --> 00:01:03.710
so that you don't disrupt today's program.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.890
And please don't record either, okay?

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.760
So, devices off.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.700
And I wanna begin today's program

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:12.370
with a land acknowledgement.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.720
Today's Chubb Fellowship event sits on

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.940
traditional indigenous territories.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.150
This includes lands of
the Mohegan, Mahican,

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.953
Mashantucket Pequot, Eastern
Pequot, Schaghticoke,

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.720
Golden Hill Paugussett,
Niantic, Quinnipiac

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.330
and other Algonquin speaking peoples.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.290
We honor and pay respect to their peoples

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.600
of past and present.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.630
So, my name is Mary Lui.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:41.180
I'm a professor in the
Department of History

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.370
and the program of American Studies,

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:46.733
and also affiliated with
ethnicity, race and migration.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.470
I'm also the head of
Timothy Dwight College,

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.820
the home of the Chubb Fellowship.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:55.830
So as the head of Timothy Dwight, or TD,

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.280
I have the honor of serving as a custodian

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:02.730
of our illustrious Chubb
Fellowship established in 1941

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:06.720
out of a prior large donation
for education purposes.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.100
This was made in 1936 by Hendon Chubb,

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.940
a graduate of the Yale class of 1895.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.300
Since its establishment,
the Chubb fund has adhered

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.300
to the goals of providing
encouragement and aid to students

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:25.330
interested in government
and American public affairs.

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.930
The fellowship, initially aimed to foster

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:32.160
among our Yale undergraduates
an interest in public service

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.230
and local and state
affairs, grew over the years

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:38.740
to include numerous distinguished
visitors in national

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.070
and international affairs, as
well as leaders in the world

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:44.330
of the arts and humanities.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.660
Since the 1940s, the Chubb
Fellowship lecture series

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:50.430
has inspired generations of Yale students

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.710
to undertake public service
and pursue leadership roles

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.300
in the hopes of creating
a better world today

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:57.920
and for posterity.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.803
The fellowship has hosted
four US presidents,

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.730
George H.W. Bush, Gerald
Ford, Jimmy Carter

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:05.830
and Harry Truman.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.760
And in recent years, we've
welcomed a wide range

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.110
of national and international leaders,

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:13.290
including our congressional
representative,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.320
the Honorable Rosa DeLauro,

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.770
Olympic ice skater, Michelle Kwan,

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.960
visual artist, Faith Ringgold,

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.350
environmental activist, Bill McKibben,

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.440
physician and humanitarian, Dr. Hawa Abdi

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.120
and world renowned actor, Shah Rukh Khan,

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.610
just to name a few.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.720
So with this quick description
of the history and aims

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:34.360
of the Chubb Fellowship,

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.430
I'm deeply honored today
to have Hilary Hahn

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:40.330
join the impressive list of Chubb fellows

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:43.800
who have visited Timothy
Dwight college and Yale.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.300
And today's event has
particularly deep significance

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.690
as it marks the relaunch of
our Chubb Fellowship program

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:52.510
after a two-year disruption,

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:55.520
because of the COVID 19 pandemic.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.510
TD had selected Ms. Hahn to
be our fall 2020 Chubb fellow

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:04.590
just before the university shut
down in the spring of 2020.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.890
And we have waited
patiently for two years,

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.640
and I heard that she's waited
patiently for two years

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:15.730
to come to campus and
join us in restarting

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.360
our wonderful Chubb Fellowship.

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.950
We've always considered
the Chubb as our gift

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:24.640
to the university and to
the city of New Haven.

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.790
So for today's Chubb event,

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:29.430
I'm particularly grateful
for the hard work

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.360
of my wonderful Timothy
Dwight College staff,

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.260
our student aids team, the
support of Yale College Arts,

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:39.360
the School of Music, and
the Yale Symphony Orchestra

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.800
for supporting our spring
Chubb Fellowship Program.

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.700
Putting together a large scale
public event is never simple.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:49.440
And doing so under COVID 19 conditions,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.610
is even more challenging.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.580
So, thank you to everyone who
helped make this happen today.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.170
I'm just simply thrilled
that we're once again

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:01.050
able to share the Chubb
Fellowship with the university

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:02.113
and the city.

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:08.113
So with that, let me
get to our main event.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:14.170
So as a preschooler, Hilary
Hahn started her musical studies

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:16.330
with the Suzuki method violin lessons

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:19.330
at the Peabody Conservatory in Baltimore.

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:21.290
At the age of 10, she was admitted

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:24.610
to the Curtis Institute
of Music in Philadelphia.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:26.980
So all you people who
are just starting violin,

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:28.961
you gotta get a move on it.

00:05:28.961 --> 00:05:30.020
(audience laughing)

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.917
At 17, she recorded her debut album

00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:35.980
"Hilary Hahn plays Bach" to wide acclaim.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:39.390
And in 2001, she was named America's best

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:42.780
young classical musician by Time Magazine.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.270
She has recorded with Decca,
Deutsche Grammophon and Sony.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:50.110
And her 19 feature albums
have all opened on the top 10

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:51.900
of the Billboard charts.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:54.740
She has won Grammys for
three of her albums,

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.500
the 2003 Brahms and
Stravinsky Concerto album,

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:02.350
a 2008 pairing of the Schoenberg
and Sibelius Concerti,

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:06.350
and her 2013 recording of In 27 Pieces,

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:09.100
the Hilary Hahn Encores.

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:11.770
Hilary Hahn is a great
supporter of new music.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.280
In 2009, she premiered
composer Jennifer Higdon's

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.130
violin concerto that
had been written for her

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.490
when the two first met in Higdon's class

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.000
in 20th Century music at Curtis.

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.710
She premiered the Higdon Concerto

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:27.050
with the Indianapolis Symphony Orchestra,

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:29.240
and the piece was awarded
the Pulitzer Prize

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:30.680
the following year.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.470
In addition to Higdon, she
has collaborated with a number

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:36.370
of contemporary composers,
commissioning new works

00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:39.770
such as Six Partidas by Anton Garcia Abril

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:44.520
and Sonata number four, Fractured
Dreams by Lera Auerbach.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.100
Timothy Dwight College selected
Ms. Hahn to be our fall 2020

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.330
Chubb fellow in recognition
of her leadership in making

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:54.790
music accessible through her
performances and recordings.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:56.790
She has launched several exciting projects

00:06:56.790 --> 00:07:01.010
such as her #100daysofpractice,
which we'll hear more about,

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:03.840
using the Instagram platform
to inspire musicians

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.950
of all levels to play and
share in their experience

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:08.340
of music making.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:11.700
She's also launched the
"Bring Your Own Baby" concerts

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.020
to encourage parents
of infants and toddlers

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:16.090
to share their love of classical music

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:18.640
in relaxed and welcoming environment.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:21.160
And in 2014, Hahn was awarded

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:24.160
the Glashutte Original
Music Festival Award

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:27.400
for her "ability to inspire young people"

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.870
through the intensive and
a compromising approach

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:31.440
to making music.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.610
In 2019, she donated her $25,000 prize

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:39.620
to the Philadelphia nonprofit,
Project 440, in recognition

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.610
of their outstanding work with
the city's youth to ignite

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:45.750
their interest in music and
harness music's potential

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.160
for individual growth and
community empowerment.

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:53.960
For those reasons, she is
our spring 2020 Chubb fellow.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:58.630
So, her energetic and innovative
leadership in expanding

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:01.850
the world of classical
music is extremely welcome,

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:06.000
especially in this present moment of 2022.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.180
So, please join me in
welcoming Hilary Hahn.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.347
(audience applauding)

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:32.910
- It's such an honor to be here.

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:35.760
Thank you for being here together,

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:38.190
all together to celebrate this.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:40.769
And I'm looking forward
to the conversation

00:08:40.769 --> 00:08:44.320
and taking some questions
from the audience.

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:47.188
First, I thought I would play
a little bit of solo Bach

00:08:47.188 --> 00:08:50.108
to get us all ready together.

00:08:50.108 --> 00:08:52.108
And then, we'll proceed.

00:08:54.873 --> 00:08:55.956
(lively violin music)

00:08:55.956 --> 00:08:57.059
New strings.

00:08:57.059 --> 00:08:58.142
(audience laughing)

00:08:58.142 --> 00:09:01.309
(lively violin music)

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:48.637
(audience applauding)

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.303
- So that was, as you heard.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.130
We are so appreciative
of you and you're...

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:17.340
Sure, I'll take some
water, if you're pouring.

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:18.400
- Yeah, sure.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:22.390
- No, that was a beautiful
performance of Bach Sarabande.

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:23.600
- Thank you.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:26.350
- Just really appreciative
of you sharing your music

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:27.560
with us today.

00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:29.393
So, maybe one more round of applause.

00:14:29.393 --> 00:14:32.560
(audience applauding)

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:41.540
So maybe just to start,
let's pick a up on Bach.

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:44.910
I know it has clearly some
very special meaning to you.

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:48.240
It was your first album, that
"Hilary Hahn Plays Bach".

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:49.490
That's quite a statement.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:52.450
But, I'm just curious
if you can tell us more

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:55.250
about your relationship
to the music of Bach.

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:59.060
- Yeah. I started my recording
career with solo Bach,

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:04.060
because I was 16 and it was the music

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:05.760
that I played the most.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:09.160
And it just felt like if
I'm gonna record something,

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:12.680
I should record something that
I've played a lot. (laughs)

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:13.670
And I'm really glad I did,

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:15.990
because starting with a solo album,

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:19.380
I got to learn all about
the recording process

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:21.900
without the pressure of time.

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:25.270
We recorded all night,
multiple nights in a row.

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:28.360
Each piece had its own
separate time of the year.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:32.440
We really had the luxury to take our time.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:33.930
And I got to learn about recording,

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:38.930
I got to learn about
playbacks and about acoustics

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:42.630
and how it sounds different under the mics

00:15:42.630 --> 00:15:45.910
from how it sounds when you're
playing for the audience.

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:48.160
So I really was working
with such an expert team.

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:50.220
And if I hadn't started with that,

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:54.590
I think I wouldn't have been
so inclined to be involved.

00:15:54.590 --> 00:15:57.220
And these days I co-produce my records,

00:15:57.220 --> 00:15:59.080
I am involved in every aspect.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:01.300
Sometimes I run the sessions.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:03.780
If it's a very small crew,

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.812
sometimes I let the
producer run the session.

00:16:06.812 --> 00:16:09.150
But I have so much experience now,

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:11.430
and I'm really glad that
I started with that.

00:16:11.430 --> 00:16:15.660
But I will say when I first proposed that,

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:17.883
as the repertoire for that first album,

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:23.210
it felt very natural to
me, but back then it was,

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:25.420
back in the '90s, this
is still 20th century.

00:16:25.420 --> 00:16:27.270
(Mary laughing)

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:30.920
Back then it was kind
of taboo for a teenager

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:35.920
to be playing Bach or be, I
suppose, projecting authority

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:40.150
on the music of Bach
or Brahms or Beethoven.

00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:44.040
It was sort of seen as further
along in your career material

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:46.140
and pretty audacious.

00:16:46.140 --> 00:16:47.900
And I think there was a little pushback

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:50.580
inside the record company,
but I didn't know that.

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:53.490
So, I just went ahead and did it. (laughs)

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:57.140
But now, I think because
I was so convinced

00:16:57.140 --> 00:17:00.370
of my playing of it, it
was so comfortable for me

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:04.600
and I enjoy the intricacies of it.

00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:06.470
It just comes very naturally to me,

00:17:06.470 --> 00:17:08.640
although it never feels
totally natural to play it.

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:12.200
But the ideas feel like they make sense

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:14.340
when I'm playing his music.

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:18.900
I think because of that, when
I was the teenager recording

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:22.590
and releasing Bach, it made it okay later.

00:17:22.590 --> 00:17:25.990
So I definitely see people
starting their recording careers

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:29.470
with solo Bach now, and
I'm like... (laughs)

00:17:30.780 --> 00:17:33.210
You're welcome. (laughs)

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:36.130
But, it was an honor to be
able to start with that.

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:38.230
- So when we were walking in,

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:43.230
we were playing your newer
album, the Virtuoso album.

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:46.281
So, lots have changed in between.

00:17:46.281 --> 00:17:49.550
- Yeah, that album is,
it's called a compilation,

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:51.456
and usually the record
companies put it together.

00:17:51.456 --> 00:17:52.289
- I see.

00:17:52.289 --> 00:17:53.920
- But I actually put it together.

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:58.920
It's my favorite tracks from
a lot of my different albums,

00:18:00.433 --> 00:18:02.340
and my favorites for different reasons.

00:18:02.340 --> 00:18:05.460
Like, I might have an emotional
attachment to one project.

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:09.440
I might remember working on
this one with the composer.

00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:12.070
Or like this, whenever
I play it as an encore,

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:15.970
it's a big hit and therefore I
really feel like the audience

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:18.040
connects with it, and
so when I listen to it

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:20.650
I remember all those great
performance experiences.

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:24.960
And so, that's what I was doing
with that record. (chuckles)

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:27.990
- So how different is that newer record

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:31.420
from the Retrospective
record, which you did in 2018.

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:33.620
Now, was that something the
record company put together

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:34.820
or you curated that too?

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:38.260
- I curated that as well,
but I added some tracks

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:40.530
that I had performed live.

00:18:40.530 --> 00:18:42.890
And so, it's kind of nice.

00:18:42.890 --> 00:18:46.980
It's nice to go back and look
at the work that you've done

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:50.530
and not have someone else pick
out their favorite things,

00:18:50.530 --> 00:18:51.433
but really put together...

00:18:51.433 --> 00:18:55.470
It's like playlists on a
really big scale. (laughs)

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:58.610
And it's kind of surprising
to me, because every time I do

00:18:58.610 --> 00:19:01.780
a project, I'm just doing that project.

00:19:01.780 --> 00:19:05.490
And when I count how
many records I've done,

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:08.050
I'm like, "How did that all happen?"

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:09.960
But it's just really
chipping away at things,

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:13.140
one little part of a project at a time

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:15.583
and having ideas that I follow through on.

00:19:16.570 --> 00:19:20.030
- So, I noticed that between
the 2018 Retrospective

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:23.580
and the 2020 Virtuoso,
there's some repeats.

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:25.917
So, clearly these are some
of your favorites, right?

00:19:25.917 --> 00:19:27.314
Can we talk about some of them?

00:19:27.314 --> 00:19:28.147
- Sure.

00:19:28.147 --> 00:19:29.650
- How did they become
some of your favorites?

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:32.290
Like the Paganini
Concerto, I think is one.

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:34.470
And the Higdon Concerto
must be another one.

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:35.303
- Mm-hmm.

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:42.083
How do I detangle the threads?

00:19:45.610 --> 00:19:48.430
Actually, I don't know if...

00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:54.470
I think of sort of the
overarching experience.

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:56.590
I think when I'm thinking
about my favorite tracks

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.690
from records, it's more
like I listen to them

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:02.210
but it's the context in which they occur.

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:07.210
So, there are pieces that
I learned with my teacher

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:08.970
and pieces that I taught myself.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:12.690
And there's all sorts of
different transitions.

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:14.750
There are pieces that I commissioned,

00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:17.720
there are projects that I started.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:22.720
And I think with Paganini
it's such virtuoso music

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:29.660
but I find the beauty in the phrasing.

00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:31.440
I kind of like looking at something

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.880
that is thought of one way and showing

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:38.430
that it's actually much
more complicated than that.

00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:42.120
There are many other aspects that exist.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:43.970
And the same with a commissioned work.

00:20:43.970 --> 00:20:46.087
I don't like to look at it as just,

00:20:46.087 --> 00:20:48.170
"Let's play the notes
and get it out there."

00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:51.950
It's really, "How can we
already work to find,"

00:20:51.950 --> 00:20:55.280
as performers, whoever the
combination of performers is

00:20:55.280 --> 00:20:58.210
for this particular
performance, "How can we find

00:20:58.210 --> 00:21:03.210
our interpretation to really
bring this music across

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:07.350
to the audience as a complete piece?"

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:10.290
If I think about what
Brahms would've sounded like

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:15.290
to a new audience, if the
performers had no idea

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:19.600
of phrasing traditions, and
then I make that leap in my head

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.890
to what we assume now of
Brahms with all the traditions,

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:24.140
we know the structure,

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:26.080
we've heard a lot of those works a lot.

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:27.910
That's my job in a new piece.

00:21:27.910 --> 00:21:31.500
My job is to do that leap
and get it to the place

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:33.320
where there are clear phrases,

00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:36.840
where the music can really
speak to the audience.

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:38.610
Not just play it through
and say it's a premier,

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:43.610
but really make it a whole
experience for the audience,

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:47.130
because when it starts that
way, it can then evolve

00:21:47.130 --> 00:21:48.773
as other players pick it up.

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:54.320
I believe the energy you put
into any piece that you play,

00:21:54.320 --> 00:21:56.300
but especially the first
performance of a piece

00:21:56.300 --> 00:21:58.210
kind of sets it on its journey.

00:21:58.210 --> 00:22:01.724
And if you give it a big,
good shove away from the dock,

00:22:01.724 --> 00:22:03.480
then you're like, "Yes."

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:07.533
And it's all set to, if it's
a ship, it's all set to sail.

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:11.590
Then that is really, I think,
the best thing you could do

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:12.840
for a new piece of music.

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:15.770
- Can you describe that in terms of...

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:18.240
Or go in a little more
detail about that in relation

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:19.830
to the Jennifer Higdon piece?

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:20.700
- Mm-hmm.

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:23.340
So I met Jennifer, as
you mentioned, in the...

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:25.950
Let's see, how old was I?

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:28.563
I was 15.

00:22:28.563 --> 00:22:29.730
So, it was the mid '90s,

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:32.420
and she had just graduated from Curtis.

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:37.050
And she hadn't yet become
inundated with commissions,

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:37.883
but it was coming.

00:22:37.883 --> 00:22:39.410
She was getting busier and busier.

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:43.477
And I was her student,

00:22:43.477 --> 00:22:46.680
and she was a really enlightening teacher.

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:48.930
She taught 20th century music history,

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:53.930
and brought it from the very
beginning of the 20th century

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:56.620
all the way into the present day.

00:22:56.620 --> 00:23:00.620
Each week, we learned about
one or two new composers

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:02.010
that we hadn't studied before.

00:23:02.010 --> 00:23:03.690
And the way she would teach,

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:07.410
she would put just like
some keywords on the board

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:08.840
and then we'd listen.

00:23:08.840 --> 00:23:12.670
And it wasn't teaching the
facts about a composer.

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:14.650
It was teaching the ideas,

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:17.450
it was teaching us what to
listen to, how to listen,

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:21.390
how to pick things apart,
but not in an analytical way,

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:23.730
but more of like an emotional connection

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:26.810
to what we were hearing and
defining that for ourselves.

00:23:26.810 --> 00:23:28.870
And there were some
facts on the board too,

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:30.570
but we were never quizzed on those.

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:35.070
We were quizzed on how
we related to the music.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:37.070
And I think that was a
great way to get to know her

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:42.070
as a composer, because she is
very specific in her music.

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:46.160
She really wants the score
to be like she writes,

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:48.590
but she doesn't write anything
extra that she doesn't need

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:49.760
to have in there.

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:52.397
And the music has a lot of energy,

00:23:52.397 --> 00:23:54.890
and it communicates immediately.

00:23:54.890 --> 00:23:57.020
It's almost clearer for
the audience than it is

00:23:57.020 --> 00:23:57.853
for the player.

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:03.340
So my challenge with that
concerto has always been

00:24:03.340 --> 00:24:07.990
to embody it as much as the
audience hears me embodying it.

00:24:07.990 --> 00:24:11.770
It's very clear music to listen
to, but in the middle of it,

00:24:11.770 --> 00:24:13.280
there's all this stuff going on.

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:16.270
So you kind of have to keep your center

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:19.360
and keep aware of all the cues,

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:22.513
but also the momentum is
super important in her music.

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:25.350
- So how much did you
work with her directly

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:27.750
to kind of bring that music to life?

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:29.310
- I like to leave composers be.

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:32.650
I like to not have them second
guessing when they're writing

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:34.250
and just write what they wanna write,

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:38.920
and then give me what they
feel they've completed.

00:24:38.920 --> 00:24:43.250
Not like the final draft, I
do like to see it (laughs)

00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:45.160
before they finalize it.

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:50.160
But basically, I just try to get the music

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:55.110
and then we talk about
things that may or may not be

00:24:55.110 --> 00:24:59.430
so practical or may or may
not communicate the way

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:01.630
that they might be
trying to communicate it.

00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:05.660
- [Mary] I'm curious.
I mean, several people

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:09.100
have now played it at this
point, so what do you hear

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:12.370
when you hear others
playing that concerto?

00:25:12.370 --> 00:25:13.203
- I haven't heard anyone else play it.

00:25:13.203 --> 00:25:14.220
- [Mary] Oh, you haven't?

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:15.730
- They play it, but I
haven't been there. (laughs)

00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:17.110
- [Mary] Oh yeah, yeah.

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:18.900
- Yeah, I would love to hear it.

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:22.830
But I think that the pieces...

00:25:22.830 --> 00:25:24.300
It has a very strong personality,

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:26.020
so I think it's gonna
sound like the same piece

00:25:26.020 --> 00:25:27.819
no matter who is playing it.

00:25:27.819 --> 00:25:28.652
- I see.

00:25:28.652 --> 00:25:31.310
- And having recorded it, I
always try to record the pieces

00:25:31.310 --> 00:25:33.440
that are written for me,
so that there's a document.

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:37.570
So that push from the doc is global,

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:39.380
so that anyone who wants to hear it,

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:41.700
anyone who wants to learn
it, there's a precedent,

00:25:41.700 --> 00:25:42.890
and they can just build on that.

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:45.250
I find it a lot easier as a player myself

00:25:45.250 --> 00:25:48.460
to have something to listen
to before I play a piece.

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:50.890
So even if a piece is
newly written for me,

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:53.680
I will make my own practice
recording and listen to it

00:25:53.680 --> 00:25:54.870
and get it in my head.

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:56.870
And that's how I develop
my interpretation.

00:25:56.870 --> 00:25:58.757
I listen to what I did and I think,

00:25:58.757 --> 00:26:01.190
"What would I wanna do
differently from that player?"

00:26:01.190 --> 00:26:03.882
And then I kind of develop it from there.

00:26:03.882 --> 00:26:06.260
- I can't help but wonder
if that's a little bit

00:26:06.260 --> 00:26:08.210
at the early Suzuki to training. (laughs)

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:09.460
- Maybe. (laughs)

00:26:09.460 --> 00:26:10.950
- Listening to the piece
before you play it.

00:26:10.950 --> 00:26:12.630
- Yeah, maybe. It could be.

00:26:12.630 --> 00:26:13.463
- Who knows?

00:26:14.350 --> 00:26:16.040
But speaking of new music,

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:19.550
you've definitely been a
big supporter of new music

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:21.320
and contemporary composers.

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:24.950
So the album 27 Pieces for example,

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:26.780
is a great example of that.

00:26:26.780 --> 00:26:28.610
So, I just wanna hear your philosophy

00:26:28.610 --> 00:26:30.220
towards contemporary music.

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:32.900
What are you looking for
when you're asking people

00:26:32.900 --> 00:26:34.933
to commission a work for you?

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:41.857
- For that album, I think I didn't realize

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:44.440
what I was taking on.

00:26:44.440 --> 00:26:49.000
I got really excited and I
commissioned a lot of people

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.450
with a lot of different styles.

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:53.640
And I was thinking in terms of minutes.

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:56.210
I was like, "Okay, I've done concerti."

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:57.530
I hadn't commissioned anything shorter

00:26:57.530 --> 00:26:58.710
than a concerto before.

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:01.730
So I was thinking, if I commission...

00:27:01.730 --> 00:27:05.170
If they're all three to five minute pieces

00:27:05.170 --> 00:27:10.170
and I commission 10, so then
10 will feel like a concerto.

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:14.400
And then it'll just be
preparing two or three concerti

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:17.164
and I can do that over time.

00:27:17.164 --> 00:27:20.360
And I didn't realize each
piece is about as much

00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:23.570
of a learning curve as an entire concerto.

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:25.810
So, it was learning.

00:27:25.810 --> 00:27:28.410
I'd only worked with two
of the composers before,

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:33.410
so it was learning the
style and the intentions

00:27:33.910 --> 00:27:38.640
and the musical language
of 27 composers, or 26.

00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:42.350
And I did an open contest
for the final encore,

00:27:42.350 --> 00:27:43.620
which was always something I wanted.

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:47.520
I wanted to open it up, have
accessibility for people

00:27:47.520 --> 00:27:49.630
whose music had not yet been recorded,

00:27:49.630 --> 00:27:51.570
that I may not have come across.

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:54.100
And I picked a winner
out of nearly 400 entries

00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:55.120
that came in.

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:57.055
So when we received almost 400 entries,

00:27:57.055 --> 00:27:59.117
I called a friend and I was like,

00:27:59.117 --> 00:28:01.620
"Can you just help sort through them?"

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:04.980
Because they were supposed
to be blind entries

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:06.890
and I didn't want anyone
to get disqualified

00:28:06.890 --> 00:28:08.760
because they'd forgotten
to take the title out

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:10.520
or forgotten to remove their name.

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:12.750
So I had some people
help me out just sort of

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:17.710
sorting through the contest,
and so I had volunteers.

00:28:17.710 --> 00:28:19.080
It was really nice.

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.040
It was a big project.

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:26.040
But doing that, it was important
to me to find composers

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:30.080
whose music I felt compelled to play.

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:33.220
I was curious when I heard their music,

00:28:33.220 --> 00:28:37.860
I was curious what they would write

00:28:37.860 --> 00:28:41.480
for a violin and piano duo,
and I just couldn't let it go.

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:44.690
So, that's my sign whenever
I'm working on an idea.

00:28:44.690 --> 00:28:46.320
If I cannot let the idea go,

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:48.143
if I can't stop talking about it,

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:50.680
even once I've convinced people.

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:52.080
They're like, "Yeah, we
get it. We're on board."

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:53.047
I'm like (mumbling).

00:28:54.930 --> 00:28:57.730
That's my sign that it's
something I have to do.

00:28:57.730 --> 00:28:59.820
I'm just not gonna be able to sleep on it

00:28:59.820 --> 00:29:01.340
and it'll fade away.

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:03.010
And so that was how I approached

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:05.080
which composers I commissioned.

00:29:05.080 --> 00:29:06.780
- And then people yes right away?

00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:09.280
'Cause I imagine, if
Hilary Hahn is writing you,

00:29:09.280 --> 00:29:11.610
yes is the answer. (laughs)

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:12.990
- Well, one of them...

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:15.120
I did a lot of cold calls,
'cause I didn't know them.

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:19.490
So I would get their number
from their agent or just,

00:29:19.490 --> 00:29:23.250
I don't know, a mutual
friend or something, someone.

00:29:23.250 --> 00:29:26.000
And I would give them a call.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:28.450
Maybe their website. (laughs)

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:31.940
And one of them thought it
was a student prank calling,

00:29:31.940 --> 00:29:33.087
so didn't call back.

00:29:33.087 --> 00:29:34.640
(Marie and audience laughing)

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:38.890
So I called again and he was
like, "Oh, sorry." (laughs)

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:41.460
- It really is you. (laughs)

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:42.960
- But it was a lot of cold calls,

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:47.147
a lot of, "Hi, I'm Hilary
Hahn. I'm doing a project.

00:29:47.147 --> 00:29:48.638
(Mary and audience laughing)

00:29:48.638 --> 00:29:49.471
Do you think you'd be interested?"

00:29:49.471 --> 00:29:51.430
And also I wasn't sure about the politics,

00:29:51.430 --> 00:29:54.370
like asking these great
contemporary composers

00:29:54.370 --> 00:29:57.210
to write short pieces for starters.

00:29:57.210 --> 00:29:59.220
There's a bit of an issue
sometimes with composers

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:01.760
being asked to write the
first piece of a program,

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:06.760
sort of the overture or being
asked to write short pieces.

00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:10.877
But it was really a
statement about the span

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:16.730
of contemporary styles
and expression through all

00:30:16.730 --> 00:30:19.240
of this range of styles.

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:21.400
That's what I was going for.

00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:25.660
And so I was hoping that the
composers would understand

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:27.380
the scope of the project.

00:30:27.380 --> 00:30:30.240
But then I wasn't sure if they
would like being on a project

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:31.843
with so many other composers.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.610
Turns out they were all really open-minded

00:30:35.610 --> 00:30:38.280
and supportive of each other,
and it was really nice.

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:41.410
Sometimes I got to introduce
two composers to each other

00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:43.770
and just sit there at post concert dinner.

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:45.430
They would both have come to the concert,

00:30:45.430 --> 00:30:47.550
because they both coincidentally
be in the same city.

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:48.710
And very different people,

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:50.140
totally different styles of music,

00:30:50.140 --> 00:30:52.850
getting to know each
other, just made so happy

00:30:52.850 --> 00:30:54.810
to introduce these creative people

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:58.200
and get to kind of observe the experience.

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:00.880
- So, you didn't really
have people coming together

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:02.970
during the writing process?

00:31:02.970 --> 00:31:05.523
- Mm-mm. And some of
them I never have met.

00:31:06.470 --> 00:31:11.470
I communicated with them by
phone, or in German. (laughs)

00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:16.670
One of them doesn't do
email, doesn't speak English,

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:18.632
doesn't have a phone.

00:31:18.632 --> 00:31:19.465
(Mary laughing)

00:31:19.465 --> 00:31:22.580
And so, his daughter speaks German

00:31:22.580 --> 00:31:25.320
and lives in another country
and can communicate with him.

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:29.060
And so he sent the music,
and then I just kind of had

00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:31.070
a couple questions about the score.

00:31:31.070 --> 00:31:33.780
So I got the answers, it's
like a game of telephone.

00:31:33.780 --> 00:31:35.720
And I was like, "Okay, I think the piece

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:38.630
is just gonna play itself then." (laughs)

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:39.463
And that's the thing,

00:31:39.463 --> 00:31:41.970
when you're a performer and
you're working with new music,

00:31:41.970 --> 00:31:45.946
you are given the responsibility
of interpreting it,

00:31:45.946 --> 00:31:47.663
bringing it to life.

00:31:50.260 --> 00:31:52.570
But there's also within
that responsibility,

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:55.970
a sense of judgment.

00:31:55.970 --> 00:31:59.430
You have to use your judgment
to figure out how closely

00:31:59.430 --> 00:32:00.700
you adhere to the score,

00:32:00.700 --> 00:32:03.770
how much you take on board
what the composer says.

00:32:03.770 --> 00:32:07.460
And ultimately, it has to
feel like the music is in you

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:09.050
and it's natural to you.

00:32:09.050 --> 00:32:12.540
So, I always try to get
the composer's feedback.

00:32:12.540 --> 00:32:14.520
Maybe I send them a practice recording.

00:32:14.520 --> 00:32:16.430
If we're in the same place,
I'll do a coaching session

00:32:16.430 --> 00:32:21.083
with them in person, and
then I'll try one more time.

00:32:21.083 --> 00:32:24.380
If I can, I'll send them
one more practice recording

00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:26.640
to see if I interpreted what they told me

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:28.510
and have incorporated it in a way

00:32:28.510 --> 00:32:30.163
that they're comfortable with.

00:32:32.150 --> 00:32:37.150
But it's definitely a process,
and you have to give over

00:32:37.240 --> 00:32:40.793
some of your creative
license for a little while.

00:32:42.190 --> 00:32:45.420
And then ultimately, you have
to understand the personality

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:46.970
of the composer.

00:32:46.970 --> 00:32:48.880
One composer will write Roberto,

00:32:48.880 --> 00:32:50.810
and mean it starts slowly and goes faster.

00:32:50.810 --> 00:32:52.240
And another will mean the opposite.

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:54.260
And there's nothing to say what they mean.

00:32:54.260 --> 00:32:55.720
To them, it's obvious.

00:32:55.720 --> 00:32:59.607
So you have to look at it,
examine every detail and say,

00:32:59.607 --> 00:33:03.480
"Now, when you say Roberto,
what does that mean to you?"

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:05.450
And sometimes it's an emotional thing.

00:33:05.450 --> 00:33:07.160
Sometimes it's very specific.

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:08.297
Sometimes it's just,

00:33:08.297 --> 00:33:11.010
"Oh, you just play it
however you wanna play it."

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:12.220
It's really interesting.

00:33:12.220 --> 00:33:16.270
And I also did a published
edition for the 27 Encores.

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:18.570
One of the things I love
about working with composers

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:23.497
is looking at the score and
just seeing how do they express

00:33:26.250 --> 00:33:28.340
their music in writing?

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:33.340
And it was such a project,
because they were doing...

00:33:33.780 --> 00:33:36.393
They were using all
different software programs.

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:40.300
Some sent in handwritten scores.

00:33:40.300 --> 00:33:42.170
Some had no bar lines.

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:43.972
So, where do you put a rehearsal number?

00:33:43.972 --> 00:33:44.805
(Mary laughing)

00:33:44.805 --> 00:33:45.890
But, it was conceptual.

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:47.483
It was always intentional.

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:51.620
And it always said something
about their creative process.

00:33:51.620 --> 00:33:54.200
So in the addition, at first,

00:33:54.200 --> 00:33:55.680
the first draft of the addition,

00:33:55.680 --> 00:34:00.680
the publisher did house standards
for all of the markings.

00:34:03.020 --> 00:34:04.950
Like, "This is where we put the slur.

00:34:04.950 --> 00:34:07.000
This is where we put dynamics."

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:09.620
Not necessarily replicating
what was in the version

00:34:09.620 --> 00:34:11.070
the composer turned in.

00:34:11.070 --> 00:34:11.947
And I looked at it and I said,

00:34:11.947 --> 00:34:13.640
"This doesn't even look
like the same piece.

00:34:13.640 --> 00:34:16.500
We have to go back to the
drawing board and start over.

00:34:16.500 --> 00:34:17.900
We have to reset all of these,

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:20.470
because they have to look
different when you flip through."

00:34:20.470 --> 00:34:23.730
If a composer was minimal,
it has to look minimal.

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:28.360
If it was a composer
writing five instructions

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:31.703
for every note, which I kid you
not is in one of the pieces,

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:36.470
everyone has to be
lined up very precisely.

00:34:36.470 --> 00:34:39.570
We can't have any floating
markings anywhere.

00:34:39.570 --> 00:34:40.970
If you're gonna do a column of five,

00:34:40.970 --> 00:34:43.010
it has to be a column of five.

00:34:43.010 --> 00:34:45.860
Not two on top, three on
the bottom, fit it wherever.

00:34:45.860 --> 00:34:47.650
Because when you're playing,
you have to look at it

00:34:47.650 --> 00:34:49.740
a certain way, and it's
an experience of reading

00:34:49.740 --> 00:34:51.440
and translating that into playing.

00:34:55.040 --> 00:34:57.470
Anyone who works with me
knows I put a lot of attention

00:34:57.470 --> 00:35:00.900
into the details, because it's important.

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:04.000
It's really important when
working with composers,

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:05.770
because you're starting a document.

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:07.403
The performance is a document.

00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:10.787
How many times do we have
discussions in rehearsal about,

00:35:10.787 --> 00:35:12.470
"What about the composer?

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:14.700
Did they mean Mezzo-Forte
here, or a piano there?"

00:35:14.700 --> 00:35:17.670
If you are premiering something
and you do an addition,

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:21.240
you're responsible in
a way for making sure

00:35:21.240 --> 00:35:23.210
if you know something about the piece

00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:25.670
and you see that it's not
showing up in the score,

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:28.270
you have to put it in there
for the sake of the composer,

00:35:28.270 --> 00:35:31.283
because no one can get everything right.

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:33.980
- Speaking of documentation,

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:36.910
I'm just curious how much
social media, like YouTube,

00:35:36.910 --> 00:35:38.090
has really changed that.

00:35:38.090 --> 00:35:41.760
So, do you feel like people
know you and your music

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:46.700
through recordings or more
through YouTube these days?

00:35:46.700 --> 00:35:47.533
I'm just curious.

00:35:47.533 --> 00:35:48.366
- That's a good question.

00:35:48.366 --> 00:35:53.135
I think that people's consumption
patterns have changed.

00:35:53.135 --> 00:35:53.968
I used to be mad at YouTube.

00:35:53.968 --> 00:35:56.050
I'm not mad at YouTube anymore.

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:58.300
- [Mary] Why were you out at YouTube?

00:35:58.300 --> 00:36:03.300
- Because, almost everything
on there was pirated

00:36:04.430 --> 00:36:08.030
and people were very proud
of spending a lot of time

00:36:08.030 --> 00:36:10.940
watching and listening to me on YouTube.

00:36:10.940 --> 00:36:14.240
And they thought they were supporting me,

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:18.683
but they actually weren't in a
business sense supporting me.

00:36:20.351 --> 00:36:22.640
And I would be trying to
sell records post concert,

00:36:22.640 --> 00:36:23.473
and people would be like,

00:36:23.473 --> 00:36:25.790
"I watch all of your YouTube videos."

00:36:25.790 --> 00:36:29.500
And I'm like, "Thank you." (laughs)

00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:30.530
But don't get me wrong,

00:36:30.530 --> 00:36:33.060
I just really appreciate the enthusiasm.

00:36:33.060 --> 00:36:35.680
And I eventually realized
that that's not really

00:36:35.680 --> 00:36:36.520
what YouTube is about.

00:36:36.520 --> 00:36:38.730
YouTube isn't about business.

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:40.303
YouTube, for me.

00:36:41.220 --> 00:36:46.220
The industry I'm in, the
performances are the business.

00:36:46.540 --> 00:36:51.200
And the recordings,
yes, it is an industry,

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:55.280
it is a business, but
as far as the purpose,

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:58.630
the purpose for me is
having the music out there

00:36:58.630 --> 00:37:00.580
as much as possible.

00:37:00.580 --> 00:37:03.400
So when I record something,
I'm recording it for a purpose.

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:06.690
I'm not recording it because
I wanna record my version.

00:37:06.690 --> 00:37:09.200
I'm recording it because
I think there's value

00:37:09.200 --> 00:37:12.580
in something I'm doing
in it existing out there

00:37:12.580 --> 00:37:14.043
for the sake of the piece.

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:16.810
And that's why I record all the new stuff.

00:37:16.810 --> 00:37:18.370
So then if it's on YouTube,

00:37:18.370 --> 00:37:19.770
if my performances are on YouTube,

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:21.240
if it makes a difference in someone's life

00:37:21.240 --> 00:37:23.030
to watch the YouTube video, it's great.

00:37:23.030 --> 00:37:25.730
'Cause I can't be
everywhere at the same time.

00:37:25.730 --> 00:37:28.220
And I do notice that
students have grown up

00:37:28.220 --> 00:37:31.800
watching my videos, so it's a
different feeling for me now.

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:34.890
I remember watching
videos that were pirated

00:37:34.890 --> 00:37:36.133
when I was a student.

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:41.530
But they were videos of Heifetz,
Elman, Milstein, Kaiser.

00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:44.930
Anything I could find
that was an older video,

00:37:44.930 --> 00:37:46.920
and they were really formative.

00:37:46.920 --> 00:37:49.150
So I just kind of look
at it differently now,

00:37:49.150 --> 00:37:54.150
and I appreciate the connective
aspect of social media,

00:37:54.650 --> 00:37:57.220
including YouTube, it's also evolved.

00:37:57.220 --> 00:37:59.510
YouTube is different, it's
a different experience.

00:37:59.510 --> 00:38:01.180
It has a different
presence in people's lives

00:38:01.180 --> 00:38:02.230
than when it started.

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:06.320
- [Mary] Yeah, along the lines
of recording and promoting

00:38:06.320 --> 00:38:08.740
different composers, I know
you've done a lot in terms of

00:38:08.740 --> 00:38:13.320
wanting to promote female
composers, composers of color,

00:38:13.320 --> 00:38:16.340
so I wanna just hear more
your philosophy about that

00:38:16.340 --> 00:38:20.023
in terms of trying to open up
the world of classical music.

00:38:21.370 --> 00:38:24.360
- I think it was actually
the 27 Encores project

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:26.920
that I'm thinking about when my philosophy

00:38:26.920 --> 00:38:31.920
on streaming changed, because
I noticed that with...

00:38:33.670 --> 00:38:35.930
When I was listening,
when I was really trying

00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:38.710
to find composers for the project,

00:38:38.710 --> 00:38:41.070
I really appreciated
the streaming services

00:38:41.070 --> 00:38:43.672
that would suggest the next thing.

00:38:43.672 --> 00:38:46.370
And that wasn't necessarily the
case back when I was putting

00:38:46.370 --> 00:38:47.250
the project together.

00:38:47.250 --> 00:38:50.249
It was about, over 10 years ago.

00:38:50.249 --> 00:38:52.889
So what we take for granted in
our listening experience now

00:38:52.889 --> 00:38:56.830
is something that has
developed in my career,

00:38:56.830 --> 00:38:58.180
in the course of my career.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:03.660
And I was able to access composers' music

00:39:03.660 --> 00:39:07.270
that wouldn't have been on
the shelf in the record store

00:39:07.270 --> 00:39:12.270
through Google searches
and through searching

00:39:12.380 --> 00:39:16.360
through various video
channels and also YouTube

00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:17.253
and all of that.

00:39:18.250 --> 00:39:21.880
At that point, that's when I
let go of my misunderstanding

00:39:21.880 --> 00:39:24.350
about the purpose of streaming.

00:39:24.350 --> 00:39:27.757
And I'm one of the artists
who actually is like,

00:39:27.757 --> 00:39:30.177
"You know what? Music
is very multifaceted."

00:39:31.060 --> 00:39:33.010
It's about reaching the audience,

00:39:33.010 --> 00:39:36.850
and it's about people
respecting the music.

00:39:36.850 --> 00:39:40.010
So, if that's what's happening
through the experience,

00:39:40.010 --> 00:39:43.010
people are getting the
emotional connection they need

00:39:43.010 --> 00:39:45.030
and hearing the voices they need to hear,

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:46.610
then that's what it's about.

00:39:46.610 --> 00:39:48.343
And in that same way,

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:54.820
I've obviously been noticing
gaps in my repertoire.

00:39:57.340 --> 00:40:00.450
I grew up in a time when at Conservatory,

00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:02.910
we really didn't learn new music at all.

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:07.500
And my teacher was born in 1907,

00:40:07.500 --> 00:40:12.500
so his contemporaries were
Barber, Bernstein, Prokofiev.

00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:19.080
And so I got the feeling
of knowing, as a performer,

00:40:19.080 --> 00:40:20.820
knowing a composer.

00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:24.990
But it was just not quite
up to date at that time.

00:40:24.990 --> 00:40:28.000
And now it's a different
experience for students.

00:40:28.000 --> 00:40:32.440
But I would say that I
definitely prioritized...

00:40:34.087 --> 00:40:38.793
I really tried with the 27
Encores to balance that project.

00:40:39.730 --> 00:40:44.730
I looked for composers from
different parts of the globe.

00:40:44.880 --> 00:40:47.630
But I realized in reexamining everything

00:40:47.630 --> 00:40:50.640
over the past few years that I thought

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:53.600
that I had good representation
in it, and I didn't.

00:40:53.600 --> 00:40:58.600
I had definitely fallen into bias gaps,

00:40:59.290 --> 00:41:04.230
and I definitely had not
taken some things on board

00:41:04.230 --> 00:41:05.630
that I should have taken on board.

00:41:05.630 --> 00:41:10.630
And so now, after all
of the racial reckoning,

00:41:11.120 --> 00:41:14.830
after George Floyd's murder,
I wound up just taking

00:41:14.830 --> 00:41:18.340
a step back because I wasn't
performing at the time anyway.

00:41:18.340 --> 00:41:21.530
So I took a step back and
I reexamined every layer

00:41:21.530 --> 00:41:22.910
of my work.

00:41:22.910 --> 00:41:27.910
And I was like, "Where
am I losing the thread?

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:32.060
Which voices are not present?

00:41:32.060 --> 00:41:33.960
How did that happen?

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:36.020
What's going on there? (chuckles)

00:41:36.020 --> 00:41:37.820
How did I arrive at this point?"

00:41:37.820 --> 00:41:42.820
And how could I go back
and be more inclusive,

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:45.200
have better representation.

00:41:45.200 --> 00:41:48.303
And I'm obviously still
learning, it's a lifelong thing.

00:41:49.180 --> 00:41:53.080
But I reexamined all of
the projects I had going.

00:41:53.080 --> 00:41:54.493
I re-shifted them.

00:41:55.450 --> 00:41:59.080
And so I think from now,
people will start to see

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:04.080
that I am putting emphasis on
representing as many voices

00:42:05.950 --> 00:42:10.220
as possible, because I
think contemporary music

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:14.460
and contemporary musical
projects are a place

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:17.070
where we can allow people to express

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:18.760
their lived experiences.

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:21.520
We look back in history,
we put things together

00:42:21.520 --> 00:42:25.520
about Mozart's life with
current events at that time.

00:42:25.520 --> 00:42:28.380
But everything being written
now, and things being written

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:30.870
by people whose voices haven't been heard,

00:42:30.870 --> 00:42:34.680
those are part of a historical document

00:42:34.680 --> 00:42:36.230
about the human experience.

00:42:36.230 --> 00:42:40.160
So we need to have people's experiences

00:42:40.160 --> 00:42:43.040
put into music and played.

00:42:43.040 --> 00:42:46.420
Not every experience can
be expressed in words.

00:42:46.420 --> 00:42:48.500
There are a lot of feelings put into music

00:42:48.500 --> 00:42:50.883
that can never be in
a historical document.

00:42:52.690 --> 00:42:55.189
A lot of people will
watch historical films

00:42:55.189 --> 00:42:57.860
to get the feel for what it
might have been like to live

00:42:57.860 --> 00:43:00.540
in that time, or what did
people speak like back then?

00:43:00.540 --> 00:43:02.700
Or, we have performance
practice and we listen.

00:43:02.700 --> 00:43:07.700
So we need to keep that musical
record going for the future,

00:43:07.850 --> 00:43:11.610
for 100 years from now,
150 years, 200 years,

00:43:11.610 --> 00:43:13.587
so that people playing
that music can be like,

00:43:13.587 --> 00:43:16.920
"That's what it felt like to
be this person at this time."

00:43:16.920 --> 00:43:20.870
And no one is a monolith,
so we need to take people

00:43:20.870 --> 00:43:24.920
as their full human selves
and allow them to say

00:43:24.920 --> 00:43:27.483
what they need to say
and get out of the way.

00:43:28.470 --> 00:43:31.810
Sharing the spotlight is not
about being in the spotlight

00:43:31.810 --> 00:43:33.454
with someone else all the time.

00:43:33.454 --> 00:43:35.400
Sometimes it's just giving
someone the spotlight

00:43:35.400 --> 00:43:38.140
and getting out of it,
and so that's something

00:43:38.140 --> 00:43:41.120
that I've been trying to
do more of these days.

00:43:41.120 --> 00:43:43.454
- So, when are we gonna see
a new project from you then?

00:43:43.454 --> 00:43:45.780
(Hilary laughing)

00:43:45.780 --> 00:43:49.330
- Well, I've already started
premiering some solo encores

00:43:49.330 --> 00:43:50.690
by composers of color.

00:43:50.690 --> 00:43:53.260
The reason I've been
commissioning solo encores

00:43:53.260 --> 00:43:57.513
is because I have complete
programming freedom with them.

00:43:58.490 --> 00:44:03.130
The short pieces I can play
in every single concert.

00:44:03.130 --> 00:44:08.130
So I can play them with an
orchestra, without the orchestra.

00:44:08.350 --> 00:44:10.120
When I would normally play solo Bach,

00:44:10.120 --> 00:44:14.170
I can play a piece by a
contemporary composer.

00:44:14.170 --> 00:44:16.550
If I'm on the radio or
I have a TV appearance,

00:44:16.550 --> 00:44:20.010
if a piece is three to four
minutes long, too long for TV,

00:44:20.010 --> 00:44:25.010
but I can program it in a lot
of short form performances.

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:29.020
It's just me and the violin,
so I can do whatever.

00:44:29.020 --> 00:44:32.930
So, that's why I'm focusing
on that solo encore form,

00:44:32.930 --> 00:44:35.690
which is something that I
missed with the duo encores.

00:44:35.690 --> 00:44:38.350
People would want to hear
them, but there's no piano

00:44:38.350 --> 00:44:40.480
on stage after I play
with orchestra. (laughs)

00:44:40.480 --> 00:44:44.020
I do a solo Bach recital,
there's no piano there.

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:47.610
So, what do I play is as an
encore? More solo Bach. (laughs)

00:44:47.610 --> 00:44:50.500
But, it's a really
interesting thing to look

00:44:50.500 --> 00:44:51.860
at the programming.

00:44:51.860 --> 00:44:54.313
I don't control most of
the programs that I'm on,

00:44:55.657 --> 00:44:56.580
I'm a guest.

00:44:56.580 --> 00:44:58.037
So, what can I do?

00:44:58.037 --> 00:45:01.027
And I think that's also
a theme of my career.

00:45:01.027 --> 00:45:03.207
"What do I really have in front of me,

00:45:03.207 --> 00:45:04.620
and what can I do with it?

00:45:04.620 --> 00:45:08.340
And how can I do more than what
I initially think I can do?"

00:45:08.340 --> 00:45:12.110
So, for example, with
the Project 440 donation,

00:45:12.110 --> 00:45:15.020
I tied that to a larger
fundraising campaign.

00:45:15.020 --> 00:45:18.340
So out of 25,000, we raised 50,000.

00:45:18.340 --> 00:45:23.340
So, looking at it like being
creative and making the most

00:45:23.470 --> 00:45:24.950
out of what you have.

00:45:24.950 --> 00:45:26.690
And then with that, they were able to hire

00:45:26.690 --> 00:45:28.150
a new full-time person.

00:45:28.150 --> 00:45:32.050
So it's kind of, also with repertoire,

00:45:32.050 --> 00:45:34.020
I look at it that way.

00:45:34.020 --> 00:45:35.550
When I have an opportunity,

00:45:35.550 --> 00:45:37.240
what can I do with that opportunity,

00:45:37.240 --> 00:45:40.913
and how can I be helpful
with that opportunity?

00:45:41.770 --> 00:45:42.870
- [Mary] That's great.

00:45:44.080 --> 00:45:44.950
You're so busy.

00:45:44.950 --> 00:45:46.420
- So, I did premier actually did...

00:45:46.420 --> 00:45:47.840
So, there are two pieces.

00:45:47.840 --> 00:45:49.310
So there's one piece by Michael Abels

00:45:49.310 --> 00:45:50.850
that I premiered during the pandemic.

00:45:50.850 --> 00:45:52.900
And there's a piece that I
just premiered the other day

00:45:52.900 --> 00:45:56.290
by Barbara (indistinct),
and she's now a big composer

00:45:57.310 --> 00:46:01.550
who lives up north in Turtle
Island, which is North America.

00:46:01.550 --> 00:46:04.380
And she wrote a lot of the
indications in the music

00:46:04.380 --> 00:46:08.420
in Ojibwe, but she also trained
in a Hochschule in Munich.

00:46:08.420 --> 00:46:13.120
So, she's got a very avant
garde European composition

00:46:13.120 --> 00:46:16.920
tradition as well as
growing up in the bush

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:19.840
and being a nature guide,
playing native flute

00:46:19.840 --> 00:46:22.913
and getting a lot of her
ideas during ceremonies.

00:46:24.090 --> 00:46:26.730
So it's really interesting, I think.

00:46:26.730 --> 00:46:31.730
We need to listen to the
voices that are creating

00:46:31.910 --> 00:46:36.910
and really put things
together in ways and programs

00:46:36.930 --> 00:46:40.993
that help us see a bigger picture.

00:46:41.920 --> 00:46:46.260
- So as you're doing this, I
know that you also rerecorded,

00:46:46.260 --> 00:46:49.020
or maybe, I say rerecorded
as though you did it before,

00:46:49.020 --> 00:46:53.540
but you recorded books one
through three of Suzuki.

00:46:53.540 --> 00:46:54.470
Is that correct?

00:46:54.470 --> 00:46:56.490
- They've had several
iterations of recordings.

00:46:56.490 --> 00:46:58.130
And so, I didn't rerecord my recording,

00:46:58.130 --> 00:47:03.130
but I recorded a new
version at there invitation.

00:47:03.770 --> 00:47:07.460
- So, what inspired you
to pick that project up?

00:47:07.460 --> 00:47:08.790
- They asked me.

00:47:08.790 --> 00:47:11.410
And, it was nice.

00:47:11.410 --> 00:47:15.280
I mean, I was the first woman
to do an official recording

00:47:15.280 --> 00:47:16.830
for the Suzuki violin repertoire.

00:47:16.830 --> 00:47:18.880
A lot of the Suzuki teachers are women,

00:47:18.880 --> 00:47:21.623
so I felt good about that.

00:47:22.890 --> 00:47:27.890
But mostly just the emotional
connection to Suzuki,

00:47:28.270 --> 00:47:29.600
because I started with it.

00:47:29.600 --> 00:47:31.960
And I remember all those pieces.

00:47:31.960 --> 00:47:34.850
When I was in the sessions,
there's a representative

00:47:34.850 --> 00:47:37.710
from Suzuki who's there as an expert,

00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:40.580
because I can't just play
the pieces however I want.

00:47:40.580 --> 00:47:43.580
I need to keep in mind how
the students need to hear them

00:47:43.580 --> 00:47:46.260
for training purposes and for speed.

00:47:46.260 --> 00:47:48.620
I can't just take off in a tempo and then-

00:47:48.620 --> 00:47:49.750
- [Mary] Just zip through book one.

00:47:49.750 --> 00:47:51.910
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:47:51.910 --> 00:47:54.280
I can't just be totally
free with all the rhythm

00:47:54.280 --> 00:47:56.730
and do full vibrato.

00:47:56.730 --> 00:47:59.050
I mean, I could, but then
it wouldn't be helpful.

00:47:59.050 --> 00:48:00.500
It would kind of be counterproductive.

00:48:00.500 --> 00:48:03.730
So, there was someone
there as a sounding board

00:48:03.730 --> 00:48:05.033
for those questions.

00:48:06.500 --> 00:48:10.270
And sometimes I would have
an instinct about the rhythm

00:48:10.270 --> 00:48:13.190
and I'd say, "Should I wait?"

00:48:13.190 --> 00:48:14.790
And they're like, "Yes,
yes. That's the tradition.

00:48:14.790 --> 00:48:17.425
You just hold that and then start the beat

00:48:17.425 --> 00:48:19.230
on the next note."

00:48:19.230 --> 00:48:22.400
And that was just so deeply embedded in me

00:48:22.400 --> 00:48:25.830
from having learned that
music a really long time ago.

00:48:25.830 --> 00:48:27.070
So, it was really very nice.

00:48:27.070 --> 00:48:29.550
And as I was recording,
I was thinking about how

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:32.990
these are the recordings that
all the little Suzuki kids

00:48:32.990 --> 00:48:34.940
and their families are
gonna be listening to.

00:48:34.940 --> 00:48:37.470
- In car rides and carpools.

00:48:37.470 --> 00:48:38.350
- Yeah.

00:48:38.350 --> 00:48:39.300
- Nonstop. (laughs)

00:48:39.300 --> 00:48:40.133
- Yeah.

00:48:41.085 --> 00:48:43.016
It was really a big honor,

00:48:43.016 --> 00:48:46.373
because that's a big responsibility,
but also really sweet.

00:48:47.730 --> 00:48:50.160
- Yeah. So, I just wanna transition maybe

00:48:50.160 --> 00:48:53.340
to some of your less traditional projects.

00:48:53.340 --> 00:48:55.930
So in terms of, I think social media,

00:48:55.930 --> 00:48:58.470
you have #100daysofpractice.

00:48:58.470 --> 00:49:00.696
I promised the audience
we'd ask about that.

00:49:00.696 --> 00:49:01.529
(Hilary laughing)

00:49:01.529 --> 00:49:02.363
And you just told me-

00:49:02.363 --> 00:49:03.196
- I just finished.

00:49:03.196 --> 00:49:05.220
- You just finished 100 days of practice.

00:49:05.220 --> 00:49:06.580
So, tell us a little bit about that.

00:49:06.580 --> 00:49:09.190
And maybe this will,
we'll start to wrap it up

00:49:09.190 --> 00:49:10.780
and open it up to the audience.

00:49:10.780 --> 00:49:11.613
- Okay.

00:49:11.613 --> 00:49:16.570
So, it began for me as a
response to a visual arts project

00:49:16.570 --> 00:49:18.660
that I saw posted on Instagram.

00:49:18.660 --> 00:49:23.020
And the suggestion from
the host of that project

00:49:23.020 --> 00:49:26.280
was just to pick a topic.

00:49:26.280 --> 00:49:29.680
So it's like 100 days of whatever
but it's work in progress,

00:49:29.680 --> 00:49:31.793
not to post your finished work.

00:49:32.700 --> 00:49:36.050
'Cause there was, at that time
with visual art on Instagram,

00:49:36.050 --> 00:49:39.560
there was an issue kind of
like of people only posting

00:49:40.580 --> 00:49:44.350
their best stuff, and it
being kind of intimidating

00:49:44.350 --> 00:49:47.493
to be there in that scene.

00:49:48.380 --> 00:49:51.770
And it was a way of opening
up the behind the scenes

00:49:51.770 --> 00:49:52.930
to be more public.

00:49:52.930 --> 00:49:54.850
And I like to draw for fun.

00:49:54.850 --> 00:49:57.330
And I was like, "Maybe I'll
just draw for 100 days."

00:49:57.330 --> 00:49:59.040
I was like, "No one wants to see that,

00:49:59.040 --> 00:50:00.634
and I don't think I'll
be drawing for 100 days.

00:50:00.634 --> 00:50:02.747
(indistinct) 100 days of drawing."

00:50:02.747 --> 00:50:06.740
So, I thought I should do a
hundred days of something...

00:50:06.740 --> 00:50:08.900
'Cause, I mean, I
desperately wanted to belong

00:50:08.900 --> 00:50:10.244
to this visual arts project.

00:50:10.244 --> 00:50:11.560
(Mary and audience laughing)

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:16.560
I was like the unpopular kid
who's trying to be in the crowd

00:50:16.640 --> 00:50:18.210
and they're like, "I'll
just do this little thing

00:50:18.210 --> 00:50:20.240
on the side, and maybe you'll notice me."

00:50:20.240 --> 00:50:21.650
- [Mary] So something
tells me if you actually

00:50:21.650 --> 00:50:24.390
posted 100 pictures,
people would look at that.

00:50:24.390 --> 00:50:25.223
- You would be surprised.

00:50:25.223 --> 00:50:26.056
- [Mary] "People, look
at what I'm drawing."

00:50:26.056 --> 00:50:27.350
- You would be surprised.

00:50:27.350 --> 00:50:28.560
It's interesting.

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:30.588
People are very into
me playing the violin.

00:50:30.588 --> 00:50:32.410
(Mary and audience laughing)

00:50:32.410 --> 00:50:36.920
So people are very kind and
tolerate my other ideas,

00:50:36.920 --> 00:50:39.160
but me playing the violin
is really what people

00:50:39.160 --> 00:50:40.093
are there for.

00:50:42.220 --> 00:50:45.630
And so I just wanted to kind
of participate in this project,

00:50:45.630 --> 00:50:47.520
but in a way that was natural to me.

00:50:47.520 --> 00:50:50.335
And so I was like, "Practice anyway.

00:50:50.335 --> 00:50:52.740
I should practice anyway.

00:50:52.740 --> 00:50:54.857
So, I'll just do that."

00:50:55.900 --> 00:50:59.100
And I didn't honestly think that anyone

00:50:59.100 --> 00:51:01.260
would care, or notice.

00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:02.740
I thought if anything,
it would be annoying,

00:51:02.740 --> 00:51:05.380
because it wasn't finished material.

00:51:05.380 --> 00:51:08.460
And we're not taught that
practice is something anyone

00:51:08.460 --> 00:51:09.880
wants to hear.

00:51:09.880 --> 00:51:13.000
If anything, we're taught that
no one wants to hear practice

00:51:13.000 --> 00:51:16.050
and that we shouldn't be playing
through like a performance

00:51:16.050 --> 00:51:17.190
when we're practicing.

00:51:17.190 --> 00:51:19.540
It should be practice, but
it's not a performance.

00:51:19.540 --> 00:51:21.340
It's not for public consumption.

00:51:21.340 --> 00:51:23.870
And so I was just thinking,
"Oh, I better be ready

00:51:23.870 --> 00:51:28.136
with some good content after
all this is over. (laughs)

00:51:28.136 --> 00:51:29.740
And for the people who stuck around."

00:51:29.740 --> 00:51:33.620
And I started getting feedback
during the first 100 days

00:51:33.620 --> 00:51:36.300
that it was really making
people feel comfortable

00:51:36.300 --> 00:51:38.176
about their own practice.

00:51:38.176 --> 00:51:40.450
And people thought it was very brave of me

00:51:40.450 --> 00:51:42.050
to post my practice.

00:51:42.050 --> 00:51:45.280
But I go on stage in front
of thousands of people

00:51:45.280 --> 00:51:46.190
and perform those.

00:51:46.190 --> 00:51:51.180
There's nothing brave about
playing not particularly well

00:51:51.180 --> 00:51:53.780
for the camera and putting it up there.

00:51:53.780 --> 00:51:57.380
It didn't, for me, translate
into an act of courage.

00:51:57.380 --> 00:52:01.700
It was just something very
inconsequential, I thought.

00:52:01.700 --> 00:52:06.700
But seeing that really made me
realize exactly how isolating

00:52:07.260 --> 00:52:10.706
practice usually is, and how
much pressure we're taught

00:52:10.706 --> 00:52:13.620
to put on ourselves in practice.

00:52:13.620 --> 00:52:18.620
I mean, I equate practicing
as a student to being told

00:52:19.880 --> 00:52:21.370
to stand in front of the mirror,

00:52:21.370 --> 00:52:23.290
pick out everything you
don't like about yourself

00:52:23.290 --> 00:52:26.990
and fix it all by yourself,
and then present yourself

00:52:26.990 --> 00:52:30.030
to a judge to see if you got better.

00:52:30.030 --> 00:52:31.160
That's really what it is.

00:52:31.160 --> 00:52:34.010
I mean, you are really
going into the practice room

00:52:34.010 --> 00:52:36.330
to find problems with your playing,

00:52:36.330 --> 00:52:37.880
to figure out how to solve them.

00:52:37.880 --> 00:52:39.080
The problems are there,
'cause you don't know

00:52:39.080 --> 00:52:40.162
how to fix them.

00:52:40.162 --> 00:52:42.720
How are you supposed to fix
something that you're doing,

00:52:42.720 --> 00:52:44.570
because you don't know how to fix it?

00:52:45.710 --> 00:52:48.760
And then there's a deadline
of a lesson or something

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:49.988
and you just...

00:52:49.988 --> 00:52:53.700
It's a really uncomfortable experience.

00:52:53.700 --> 00:52:56.940
And I have arrived at
a place where, for me,

00:52:56.940 --> 00:53:01.940
practice is about experimenting,
finding solutions.

00:53:02.300 --> 00:53:05.020
You can't force something
that's not ready to happen.

00:53:05.020 --> 00:53:09.120
So just meandering around
it, trying this, trying that.

00:53:09.120 --> 00:53:12.900
And all the while I do have
very high pressure deadlines,

00:53:12.900 --> 00:53:16.830
however whatever I do
in the practice room,

00:53:20.001 --> 00:53:25.001
that's as far as I will get
with that piece at that time.

00:53:25.180 --> 00:53:28.650
Then I go out and I play it
and I learn something on stage

00:53:28.650 --> 00:53:30.550
and I'm like, "Okay,
there's something else

00:53:30.550 --> 00:53:32.020
I need to figure out.

00:53:32.020 --> 00:53:33.800
Let me just do what I can."

00:53:33.800 --> 00:53:34.633
Perform it.

00:53:35.937 --> 00:53:37.430
"That didn't work. Let's see.

00:53:37.430 --> 00:53:39.270
Let's try this little other idea."

00:53:39.270 --> 00:53:43.400
And that's been such a
healthier process for me.

00:53:43.400 --> 00:53:46.090
I think about the pressure
of practicing every day

00:53:46.090 --> 00:53:47.450
from when we're little.

00:53:47.450 --> 00:53:49.500
We were talking about this earlier.

00:53:49.500 --> 00:53:52.520
There's nothing I do every day of my life

00:53:52.520 --> 00:53:54.173
besides survival things.

00:53:55.580 --> 00:53:58.580
I don't wanna watch my favorite
movie 100 days in a row.

00:53:58.580 --> 00:54:03.230
I don't do anything the same
for 365 days of the year,

00:54:03.230 --> 00:54:08.010
except I do, fortunately,
have a place to live.

00:54:08.010 --> 00:54:11.100
I have a bed to sleep
in, I have food to eat.

00:54:11.100 --> 00:54:13.483
Those are the things I do every day.

00:54:14.470 --> 00:54:16.550
And not everyone has access to that,

00:54:16.550 --> 00:54:17.830
so I recognize that privilege.

00:54:17.830 --> 00:54:20.930
But practicing is not a survival skill.

00:54:20.930 --> 00:54:22.410
I think we need to keep that in mind.

00:54:22.410 --> 00:54:27.410
It's a lifelong experiment,
and it's a lifelong pursuit.

00:54:27.860 --> 00:54:32.520
And it's for you to find out
who you are as a musician

00:54:32.520 --> 00:54:35.380
without anyone watching you.

00:54:35.380 --> 00:54:37.200
The pressure is off
when you're practicing.

00:54:37.200 --> 00:54:38.950
That's what practice should be.

00:54:38.950 --> 00:54:42.130
And so the more I realize that
by reading people's comments,

00:54:42.130 --> 00:54:45.200
doing these 100 days of practice seasons,

00:54:45.200 --> 00:54:48.760
the more I realize how important
it is to talk about that.

00:54:48.760 --> 00:54:50.610
Take the pressure off.

00:54:50.610 --> 00:54:54.070
It's your time to do your thing your way.

00:54:54.070 --> 00:54:57.490
- So that realization only
is recent for you then,

00:54:57.490 --> 00:55:01.060
because the 100 days started
about five, six years ago.

00:55:01.060 --> 00:55:02.480
- Mm-hmm.

00:55:02.480 --> 00:55:06.960
Yeah. I think also
watching myself practice

00:55:06.960 --> 00:55:10.130
and seeing how I work, 'cause I didn't see

00:55:10.130 --> 00:55:11.523
how I worked before.

00:55:13.390 --> 00:55:15.950
And also just growing into
who I am as a musician.

00:55:15.950 --> 00:55:19.810
I've always been trying to find my flow

00:55:19.810 --> 00:55:23.273
even when I was already
performing full time.

00:55:25.640 --> 00:55:28.250
Yeah, always trying to
align what I wanted to do

00:55:28.250 --> 00:55:30.290
with what I was actually doing.

00:55:30.290 --> 00:55:33.270
What do I wanna communicate,
versus what people

00:55:33.270 --> 00:55:35.070
are hearing from me.

00:55:35.070 --> 00:55:37.170
So, that was what I did in practice.

00:55:37.170 --> 00:55:38.720
That was what practice was for.

00:55:40.000 --> 00:55:42.880
- So, probably the last
question and then we'll open up

00:55:42.880 --> 00:55:44.910
to the floor is that, this sounds...

00:55:44.910 --> 00:55:47.650
You just described yourself
in such a serious way.

00:55:47.650 --> 00:55:50.133
And it just seems like
maybe some of the videos

00:55:50.133 --> 00:55:51.709
and some of the projects you've done

00:55:51.709 --> 00:55:54.350
are not quite as serious.

00:55:54.350 --> 00:55:58.180
So, for example, you appearing
on stage with TwoSet Violin

00:55:58.180 --> 00:56:01.140
hula hooping to and playing Paganini.

00:56:01.140 --> 00:56:02.416
- But I double-octaved it.

00:56:02.416 --> 00:56:04.550
(Mary laughing)

00:56:04.550 --> 00:56:06.720
- How many of you have seen that?

00:56:06.720 --> 00:56:09.000
(Hilary laughing)

00:56:09.000 --> 00:56:11.770
So that definitely tells me you
have a great sense of humor.

00:56:11.770 --> 00:56:15.150
And we've been laughing
throughout all of today.

00:56:15.150 --> 00:56:16.920
- I take my work really seriously.

00:56:16.920 --> 00:56:18.750
It's a big responsibility.

00:56:18.750 --> 00:56:22.900
And there are a lot of
people who save up money

00:56:22.900 --> 00:56:24.654
to buy tickets for concerts.

00:56:24.654 --> 00:56:28.380
They save up money to buy records.

00:56:28.380 --> 00:56:31.640
A lot of people give their
time to listen to my work,

00:56:31.640 --> 00:56:36.093
and I take it really
seriously just to do my best

00:56:36.093 --> 00:56:40.870
to create an emotional connection
and emotional experience

00:56:40.870 --> 00:56:44.920
and not to have anything
get in the way of that.

00:56:44.920 --> 00:56:47.500
And that's why I work really hard.

00:56:47.500 --> 00:56:49.200
But I love music.

00:56:49.200 --> 00:56:50.790
I love performing.

00:56:50.790 --> 00:56:54.280
I love that feeling of
connecting with everyone.

00:56:54.280 --> 00:56:56.100
I look at music as a public service,

00:56:56.100 --> 00:56:57.620
even though it's a business.

00:56:57.620 --> 00:57:00.100
It's called an industry,
but it's something

00:57:00.100 --> 00:57:02.670
that we can provide for each other.

00:57:02.670 --> 00:57:06.753
I get things from the audience
that I don't get from myself

00:57:08.480 --> 00:57:09.500
in normal life.

00:57:09.500 --> 00:57:14.500
And I hope I give things to
the audience in performance.

00:57:14.730 --> 00:57:19.200
I give feelings and
experiences and moments

00:57:19.200 --> 00:57:20.563
with my colleagues.

00:57:21.470 --> 00:57:24.770
I hope that those are
meaningful to people.

00:57:24.770 --> 00:57:28.750
And when you get comments
from people like...

00:57:28.750 --> 00:57:30.960
Well, you see someone
when they're a student,

00:57:30.960 --> 00:57:34.080
like years ago, I would meet
someone who's kind of my age,

00:57:34.080 --> 00:57:36.340
like a teenager, I meet
them after a concert.

00:57:36.340 --> 00:57:39.780
And then 10 years later
they're coming to the concert,

00:57:39.780 --> 00:57:41.750
and then they bring their baby.

00:57:41.750 --> 00:57:43.830
And then the kid plays an instrument.

00:57:43.830 --> 00:57:45.060
10 years later, they're at the...

00:57:45.060 --> 00:57:47.230
And it's nice.

00:57:47.230 --> 00:57:49.630
You realize that you're
part of people's lives

00:57:49.630 --> 00:57:51.220
and they really...

00:57:52.520 --> 00:57:55.140
There's a role that play in someone's life

00:57:55.140 --> 00:57:58.250
and you don't exactly know
what's meaningful to whom

00:57:58.250 --> 00:58:02.970
at which times, so I take every
performance really seriously

00:58:02.970 --> 00:58:07.883
because in every concert
there's someone experiencing

00:58:09.150 --> 00:58:11.440
my current feeling.

00:58:11.440 --> 00:58:13.850
So if I'm honest with my playing,

00:58:13.850 --> 00:58:17.550
and I really express my
feeling, that person who needs

00:58:17.550 --> 00:58:22.550
that format to understand
that feeling in themselves

00:58:23.010 --> 00:58:24.950
or acknowledge that feeling themselves,

00:58:24.950 --> 00:58:27.890
that won't be really meaningful for them.

00:58:27.890 --> 00:58:32.060
There was a concert
recently where, I think,

00:58:32.060 --> 00:58:36.067
my colleague and I were super
exhausted and we're like,

00:58:36.067 --> 00:58:37.739
"We're gonna get it
together for the concert.

00:58:37.739 --> 00:58:38.977
We're gonna do it."

00:58:39.850 --> 00:58:41.630
And then I was like, "I
bet you there's someone

00:58:41.630 --> 00:58:46.280
in the audience tonight who
is celebrating a birthday."

00:58:46.280 --> 00:58:49.127
And then sure enough after
the concert, someone was like,

00:58:49.127 --> 00:58:52.920
"My parents got me this ticket
a year ago for my birthday,

00:58:52.920 --> 00:58:56.400
and I've been looking forward
to it, and here we are."

00:58:56.400 --> 00:59:01.400
I've had people tell me that
this particular recording

00:59:01.600 --> 00:59:06.489
was playing in their
parents' room during hospice,

00:59:06.489 --> 00:59:09.240
or their baby was born to this music.

00:59:09.240 --> 00:59:13.530
And I'm like, "I'm just one person."

00:59:13.530 --> 00:59:16.490
But there's this whole continuum of music,

00:59:16.490 --> 00:59:18.550
and it's really meaningful.

00:59:18.550 --> 00:59:21.660
And to be given the
opportunity to communicate

00:59:21.660 --> 00:59:22.780
through this music,

00:59:22.780 --> 00:59:26.560
it's a really big deal
that I don't take lightly.

00:59:26.560 --> 00:59:30.780
So even in the TwoSet
thing, I was practicing it.

00:59:30.780 --> 00:59:33.910
I am maybe not the best hula hooper,

00:59:33.910 --> 00:59:35.730
but I can keep it going.

00:59:35.730 --> 00:59:36.950
I practiced the hula hooping.

00:59:36.950 --> 00:59:38.820
I didn't just go out there and do it.

00:59:38.820 --> 00:59:41.620
I practiced at home with Paganini.

00:59:41.620 --> 00:59:43.970
I practiced my Paganini.

00:59:43.970 --> 00:59:47.110
I don't just walk in and toss things off,

00:59:47.110 --> 00:59:50.560
but I also love spontaneity
and I love the moment.

00:59:50.560 --> 00:59:53.030
So, I just always try to be
prepared for those moments

00:59:53.030 --> 00:59:55.270
so that when something important happens,

00:59:55.270 --> 00:59:59.973
I can just step into it and
absorb all that feeling.

01:00:00.810 --> 01:00:02.790
- Well, I have to say it
was really, really fun

01:00:02.790 --> 01:00:03.760
to be in the audience.

01:00:03.760 --> 01:00:05.980
And all of a sudden there
you were with the hula hoop.

01:00:05.980 --> 01:00:08.440
- No one knew I was watching
the show beforehand either.

01:00:08.440 --> 01:00:09.510
- No, we did not know. (laughs)

01:00:09.510 --> 01:00:11.956
And everyone around
was losing their minds.

01:00:11.956 --> 01:00:12.789
(Hilary laughing)

01:00:12.789 --> 01:00:15.096
"Oh my God that's Hilary
Hahn with a hula hoop.

01:00:15.096 --> 01:00:17.870
What is she gonna do with a hula hoop?"

01:00:17.870 --> 01:00:21.600
- It's funny when your name
becomes like an expression.

01:00:21.600 --> 01:00:23.613
Like, "Hilary Hahn."

01:00:23.613 --> 01:00:28.570
I don't go through life, like
I'm walking down the hall,

01:00:28.570 --> 01:00:29.740
I'm like, "I'm Hilary Hahn."

01:00:29.740 --> 01:00:32.980
(Mary and audience laughing)

01:00:32.980 --> 01:00:35.357
But everyone is like,
"That's Hilary Hahn."

01:00:36.262 --> 01:00:37.095
- That just happened down here.

01:00:37.095 --> 01:00:37.928
- "I'm Hilary, hello."

01:00:37.928 --> 01:00:39.180
Or, "Ms. Hahn."

01:00:39.180 --> 01:00:41.810
For me, my name is like
two separate words.

01:00:41.810 --> 01:00:45.003
It's like, TwoSet Violin, Hilary Hahn.

01:00:46.017 --> 01:00:47.043
"Is that Hilary Hahn?"

01:00:47.909 --> 01:00:49.500
"That's Hilary Hahn."

01:00:49.500 --> 01:00:50.333
It's funny.

01:00:50.333 --> 01:00:53.563
It's really cute, I really
appreciate the acknowledgement.

01:00:54.520 --> 01:00:56.760
- I think on that fun
note, let's let's pause

01:00:56.760 --> 01:00:58.620
and let's hear from the audience.

01:00:58.620 --> 01:01:03.423
So I do have two of our wonderful
aides, or is it just one?

01:01:04.880 --> 01:01:05.910
Okay.

01:01:05.910 --> 01:01:07.580
So while they get situated...

01:01:07.580 --> 01:01:10.573
So it's COVID, so we're
not passing the mic.

01:01:12.089 --> 01:01:14.903
I mean, if you can go to the
other side, that'd be great.

01:01:16.020 --> 01:01:21.020
And then I think the idea then
is, if you have a question,

01:01:21.460 --> 01:01:24.550
raise your hand and either
Andrew or May will come.

01:01:24.550 --> 01:01:28.120
And just speak the question
that one of them will speak

01:01:28.120 --> 01:01:29.260
into the microphone.

01:01:29.260 --> 01:01:30.463
So, don't pass the mic.

01:01:33.430 --> 01:01:37.090
Yeah, so just tell him
the question and then...

01:01:37.090 --> 01:01:39.580
And May, you'll speak into the microphone.

01:01:39.580 --> 01:01:40.413
- [May] Oh, I see.

01:01:40.413 --> 01:01:41.246
- Got it.

01:01:41.246 --> 01:01:42.470
- We have a human amplifier.

01:01:42.470 --> 01:01:44.300
- We're trying to be COVID safe.

01:01:44.300 --> 01:01:45.930
So, you're just holding the microphone.

01:01:45.930 --> 01:01:46.880
- No, no, wait.

01:01:46.880 --> 01:01:47.890
I got it, I got it.

01:01:47.890 --> 01:01:49.715
Everyone, sit down.

01:01:49.715 --> 01:01:51.254
(Mary laughing)

01:01:51.254 --> 01:01:52.340
You can sit down.

01:01:52.340 --> 01:01:53.370
Thank you.

01:01:53.370 --> 01:01:58.340
And then you say your question
to your human amplifier,

01:01:58.340 --> 01:01:59.173
who then has a microphone.

01:01:59.173 --> 01:02:00.040
- And he will repeat.

01:02:00.040 --> 01:02:00.930
- Yes.

01:02:00.930 --> 01:02:01.763
Thank you.

01:02:03.550 --> 01:02:06.570
- [Andrew] While we're over
here, Alvin here has a question.

01:02:06.570 --> 01:02:07.403
- Great.

01:02:07.403 --> 01:02:08.630
- Okay, your next. (laughs)

01:02:08.630 --> 01:02:11.360
- [Andrew] So Alvin asks,
if you've ever had any times

01:02:11.360 --> 01:02:13.690
that you've felt unnerving to play.

01:02:13.690 --> 01:02:15.940
Maybe even a little
bit nervous even though

01:02:15.940 --> 01:02:19.830
you've practiced a lot, even
despite performing so often

01:02:19.830 --> 01:02:21.623
over your career.

01:02:23.200 --> 01:02:26.420
Or unmotivated, is a
caveat to the question,

01:02:26.420 --> 01:02:28.013
unmotivated to practice.

01:02:28.013 --> 01:02:28.943
- Unmotivated.

01:02:30.830 --> 01:02:32.370
Would you like me to
talk about the practice

01:02:32.370 --> 01:02:34.183
or the performance experience?

01:02:36.851 --> 01:02:39.510
- [Andrew] It was more so
being unmotivated to play

01:02:39.510 --> 01:02:41.950
and just not feeling like
you were playing with feeling

01:02:41.950 --> 01:02:43.570
and emotions even though
you've been playing

01:02:43.570 --> 01:02:44.760
for such a long time.

01:02:44.760 --> 01:02:46.160
So the jist is (indistinct).

01:02:49.220 --> 01:02:51.270
- Okay. I did hear that one.

01:02:51.270 --> 01:02:52.410
- Okay, great.

01:02:52.410 --> 01:02:54.700
- So, how do I continue
to play with feeling

01:02:54.700 --> 01:02:55.900
even though I'm unmotivated?

01:02:55.900 --> 01:02:58.550
This is something I've
been working on constantly

01:02:58.550 --> 01:03:01.230
all of my life as a musician.

01:03:01.230 --> 01:03:04.130
And I think I finally landed on something.

01:03:04.130 --> 01:03:07.730
So, maybe I can give you a
little tip to leapfrog you over

01:03:07.730 --> 01:03:09.783
20 years of figuring it out.

01:03:11.130 --> 01:03:14.363
I play what I'm feeling in the moment.

01:03:17.401 --> 01:03:22.150
I used to think that I should
plan it, I should practice it.

01:03:22.150 --> 01:03:23.730
This is how I want to play.

01:03:23.730 --> 01:03:28.080
My interpretation of something
was a series of decisions

01:03:28.080 --> 01:03:33.080
and a style, but the problem
was I kept getting reviews,

01:03:33.230 --> 01:03:37.390
comments on the YouTube videos
like, "She sounds robotic.

01:03:37.390 --> 01:03:38.690
It's not expressive."

01:03:38.690 --> 01:03:41.170
And I'm like, "I'm
pouring myself into this.

01:03:41.170 --> 01:03:43.610
What's not connecting?"

01:03:43.610 --> 01:03:46.810
I would get reviews constantly
about great technique,

01:03:46.810 --> 01:03:49.200
but kind of reserved musicality.

01:03:49.200 --> 01:03:52.360
And I just was like a puddle
at the end of the concert.

01:03:52.360 --> 01:03:55.450
I was so worn out and I was forcing

01:03:55.450 --> 01:03:57.883
all of the possible emotion in.

01:03:59.990 --> 01:04:03.140
It was only when I started
doing solo Bach recitals,

01:04:03.140 --> 01:04:04.810
for the first time, like a few years ago.

01:04:04.810 --> 01:04:06.480
I never played a solo recital before,

01:04:06.480 --> 01:04:08.820
because I loved working with people,

01:04:08.820 --> 01:04:12.060
and I didn't feel the need
to do a full solo recital.

01:04:12.060 --> 01:04:14.123
But when I started doing it as a project,

01:04:16.560 --> 01:04:20.650
I realized that I was
second-guessing myself too much

01:04:20.650 --> 01:04:21.920
to get through a concert.

01:04:21.920 --> 01:04:25.100
I just was not feeling like I was...

01:04:25.100 --> 01:04:27.130
I was in a very uncomfortable
psychological state

01:04:27.130 --> 01:04:31.310
when it was just me and I
was trying to do a thing.

01:04:31.310 --> 01:04:33.840
I was trying to do this,
I was trying to do that.

01:04:33.840 --> 01:04:36.800
Always judging what I
was doing as I was going.

01:04:36.800 --> 01:04:39.790
And I just, on the third concert I decided

01:04:41.230 --> 01:04:42.110
I'm just gonna stay...

01:04:42.110 --> 01:04:44.080
I was working on this in the
masterclass a little bit today.

01:04:44.080 --> 01:04:45.220
So, this is a little bit of background

01:04:45.220 --> 01:04:46.393
on how I arrived there.

01:04:47.530 --> 01:04:51.070
So I just decided I'm just
gonna stay on these notes

01:04:51.070 --> 01:04:54.990
until I feel like I'm done
with them, the whole concert.

01:04:54.990 --> 01:04:58.490
Every note I'm gonna be in it
as long as I need to be in it.

01:04:58.490 --> 01:05:01.620
Whether that's shorter than I
planned, longer than I planned

01:05:01.620 --> 01:05:06.110
slower, faster, in good
taste, not in good taste.

01:05:06.110 --> 01:05:09.340
I am just going to enjoy every
note and not think ahead,

01:05:09.340 --> 01:05:11.940
not think back for an hour and a half.

01:05:11.940 --> 01:05:15.530
And it was so cleansing.

01:05:15.530 --> 01:05:20.530
And so, also healing as a musician.

01:05:21.400 --> 01:05:23.496
You're trying, trying, trying,
trying, trying, trying.

01:05:23.496 --> 01:05:25.890
You're analyzing yourself,
trying to get better,

01:05:25.890 --> 01:05:28.327
trying to do this, trying to
do that, trying to do that.

01:05:28.327 --> 01:05:31.640
"Did I do it? Did I not do it?
How good was that concert?"

01:05:31.640 --> 01:05:34.560
Just to be in every note as it went by.

01:05:34.560 --> 01:05:37.170
And it took me all of
those decades of experience

01:05:37.170 --> 01:05:40.010
to be able to do that
and recognize when I was

01:05:40.010 --> 01:05:43.900
in the right note at the
right time, for myself.

01:05:43.900 --> 01:05:46.850
But, the reviews were entirely different.

01:05:46.850 --> 01:05:50.620
The feedback on my
social media commentary,

01:05:50.620 --> 01:05:51.600
totally different.

01:05:51.600 --> 01:05:54.410
And I was like, "That's what
I needed to do all along."

01:05:54.410 --> 01:05:59.410
I needed to be in my space
emotionally, whatever that was.

01:05:59.910 --> 01:06:04.910
And so now, instead of working
against whatever's happening

01:06:05.650 --> 01:06:08.860
in my day, that idea,
"The show must go on."

01:06:08.860 --> 01:06:12.360
The show must go on,
because we feel things,

01:06:12.360 --> 01:06:15.030
not despite things going wrong.

01:06:15.030 --> 01:06:16.570
You have a flu and you go...

01:06:16.570 --> 01:06:17.930
Don't take this.

01:06:17.930 --> 01:06:20.540
Don't go work with COVID, whatever.

01:06:20.540 --> 01:06:24.360
But when I was growing up,
it's like, "Are you sick?

01:06:24.360 --> 01:06:25.400
Can you perform?"

01:06:25.400 --> 01:06:28.330
If you can, you go out and
do it despite being sick.

01:06:28.330 --> 01:06:31.167
Now, it would be like, "Am I low energy?"

01:06:32.300 --> 01:06:35.820
The show must go on
because I'm low energy,

01:06:35.820 --> 01:06:38.520
and I'm gonna change my energy
in the course of the show.

01:06:38.520 --> 01:06:41.580
So, I'll start performing low energy.

01:06:41.580 --> 01:06:44.250
I'll be like, "Just not a
day where I'm gonna play

01:06:44.250 --> 01:06:45.210
loud right now.

01:06:45.210 --> 01:06:46.820
I don't have fast in me.

01:06:46.820 --> 01:06:49.560
I don't have dynamic in me.

01:06:49.560 --> 01:06:51.620
I'm going to really
take this energy down."

01:06:51.620 --> 01:06:54.223
Even if it's the beginning
of the Brahms Concerto,

01:06:55.230 --> 01:06:57.690
slow it down, take your time.

01:06:57.690 --> 01:07:00.570
And then at some point,
you've identified with

01:07:00.570 --> 01:07:03.210
that low energy and you're like,
"I'm sick of it. Let's go."

01:07:03.210 --> 01:07:06.840
And then, then it picks up
and you've completely gone

01:07:06.840 --> 01:07:11.580
through your own evolution
with the audience.

01:07:11.580 --> 01:07:15.140
And you arrived honestly
at a different mood,

01:07:15.140 --> 01:07:17.490
and everyone has been there with you.

01:07:17.490 --> 01:07:21.000
And that I think is what is motivating.

01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:24.527
Music is this channel
for you rather than this,

01:07:24.527 --> 01:07:29.100
"Despite this, I have to do that."

01:07:29.100 --> 01:07:31.530
If you can work with it, it's amazing.

01:07:31.530 --> 01:07:35.270
And it's that thing, like
whatever you're going through,

01:07:35.270 --> 01:07:38.390
there's someone out there
who hears it and feels it

01:07:38.390 --> 01:07:39.690
and needs that.

01:07:39.690 --> 01:07:42.480
So the more you can just
be true to yourself.

01:07:42.480 --> 01:07:43.930
Obviously not...

01:07:45.480 --> 01:07:48.080
You're working with colleagues,
you want to find a way

01:07:48.080 --> 01:07:52.270
to connect with colleagues
and do that same

01:07:52.270 --> 01:07:54.510
emotional honesty while playing.

01:07:54.510 --> 01:07:58.490
I think that it is really a
very, very special conversation

01:07:58.490 --> 01:07:59.323
at that point.

01:08:00.370 --> 01:08:02.330
You could do that in practice too.

01:08:02.330 --> 01:08:05.660
Just take a moment and,
"Where am I emotionally?

01:08:05.660 --> 01:08:08.030
Why do I feel this resistance?

01:08:08.030 --> 01:08:08.863
What is going on?"

01:08:08.863 --> 01:08:11.020
And don't fix it, just be with that.

01:08:11.020 --> 01:08:14.790
Just notice, it's that
idea, notice what's going on

01:08:14.790 --> 01:08:15.950
and just say, "Okay."

01:08:15.950 --> 01:08:18.460
Name it, notice it,
name it, leave it alone.

01:08:18.460 --> 01:08:19.780
Just do your thing.

01:08:19.780 --> 01:08:23.340
And I think it, then it's
not a matter of motivation,

01:08:23.340 --> 01:08:25.663
it's a matter of living through it.

01:08:28.320 --> 01:08:30.055
I think we had this question.

01:08:30.055 --> 01:08:31.480
You've been so patient.

01:08:31.480 --> 01:08:32.340
Now you can stand up.

01:08:32.340 --> 01:08:33.440
Now you understand what's happening.

01:08:33.440 --> 01:08:36.118
If you want to stand up,
you can stand up. (laughs)

01:08:36.118 --> 01:08:37.239
- [Ray] Hi, Hilary.
- Hey.

01:08:37.239 --> 01:08:38.072
- [Ray] Can you hear me okay?
- Yeah.

01:08:38.072 --> 01:08:39.408
- Yeah, you have a big voice.

01:08:39.408 --> 01:08:40.241
- [Ryan] I'm Ryan.

01:08:40.241 --> 01:08:43.160
I'm a first year undergrad at
Yale, and I play the violin,

01:08:43.160 --> 01:08:44.956
so this topic is especially
meaningful for me.

01:08:44.956 --> 01:08:45.789
- Great.

01:08:45.789 --> 01:08:46.622
- [Ray] Thank you so much
for being here with us.

01:08:46.622 --> 01:08:47.455
- Thank you.

01:08:47.455 --> 01:08:49.830
- [Ryan] I was just
wondering for those students

01:08:49.830 --> 01:08:52.900
who might not necessarily be
pursuing a professional career

01:08:52.900 --> 01:08:55.640
playing music, how can we still keep music

01:08:55.640 --> 01:08:56.940
a daily part of our lives?

01:08:58.270 --> 01:09:01.070
- This may be my own inclination,

01:09:01.070 --> 01:09:06.070
but I think we can all make
the world better by connecting

01:09:07.400 --> 01:09:10.580
and connecting with people we know,

01:09:10.580 --> 01:09:12.700
connecting with people we don't know.

01:09:12.700 --> 01:09:15.600
Music can be a conduit
for a lot of things.

01:09:15.600 --> 01:09:20.600
So, I find that when I need to
keep things moving musically,

01:09:23.480 --> 01:09:24.313
despite a lot...

01:09:24.313 --> 01:09:25.670
'Cause a lot of my work has nothing to do

01:09:25.670 --> 01:09:27.210
with playing the violin.

01:09:27.210 --> 01:09:30.610
So, I'm always trying to
find time to practice.

01:09:30.610 --> 01:09:32.820
And that's actually why 100
days of practice is good,

01:09:32.820 --> 01:09:37.820
'cause then the work is
practicing. Yes! (laughs)

01:09:37.940 --> 01:09:40.380
But when you don't have a lot of time,

01:09:40.380 --> 01:09:43.270
maybe have an overarching goal.

01:09:43.270 --> 01:09:45.040
What are you gonna do with the music?

01:09:45.040 --> 01:09:46.547
What is the music gonna mean for you?

01:09:46.547 --> 01:09:49.770
What's it gonna mean to the
other people in your life?

01:09:49.770 --> 01:09:51.140
What's it gonna mean to the community?

01:09:51.140 --> 01:09:53.640
You can maybe put together
just a little goal

01:09:53.640 --> 01:09:55.540
of some community project.

01:09:55.540 --> 01:09:59.220
And then the more you
collaborate with people,

01:09:59.220 --> 01:10:03.470
the more you'll be forced
to prioritize the music.

01:10:03.470 --> 01:10:06.283
And also you'll get
that feedback loop from,

01:10:07.440 --> 01:10:09.740
when you're in school, you
have performance opportunities.

01:10:09.740 --> 01:10:12.260
When you're not, you may not
have performance opportunities.

01:10:12.260 --> 01:10:16.230
So create ones that make a
difference for some other people,

01:10:16.230 --> 01:10:19.530
and then it's not so much about delivering

01:10:19.530 --> 01:10:21.630
the perfect performance from the stage.

01:10:21.630 --> 01:10:24.640
It's about the purpose of music.

01:10:24.640 --> 01:10:28.331
Everyone has individual ideas,
so your idea will be valid

01:10:28.331 --> 01:10:33.331
and you'll be able to feel like
there's a real point to it,

01:10:33.910 --> 01:10:35.630
which is very motivating
when you don't have

01:10:35.630 --> 01:10:37.710
a lot of time as well.

01:10:37.710 --> 01:10:40.723
That would be my suggestion. (chuckles)

01:10:44.602 --> 01:10:45.435
- [Student] Hi.
- Hi.

01:10:45.435 --> 01:10:48.329
- [Student] I'm also a first
year at Yale on my undergrad.

01:10:48.329 --> 01:10:50.079
I'm not a violinist, but I've played piano

01:10:50.079 --> 01:10:51.408
for the last 13 years.

01:10:51.408 --> 01:10:55.320
But for me, in my
experience, (indistinct) work

01:10:55.320 --> 01:10:57.715
goes into character content.

01:10:57.715 --> 01:11:00.420
Even no matter how stressed
you are (indistinct) itself,

01:11:00.420 --> 01:11:01.720
it's still a lot fun.

01:11:01.720 --> 01:11:03.560
So my question is gonna be,

01:11:03.560 --> 01:11:05.938
what is your favorite live
performance experience?

01:11:05.938 --> 01:11:08.771
Performance experience you've had.

01:11:09.830 --> 01:11:10.706
- Ever?

01:11:10.706 --> 01:11:12.779
(Mary and audience laughing)

01:11:12.779 --> 01:11:14.076
- [Student] Ever, or
in the last six months.

01:11:14.076 --> 01:11:16.701
Whatever (indistinct) timeframe.

01:11:16.701 --> 01:11:19.700
(Mary and audience laughing)

01:11:19.700 --> 01:11:21.600
- I'm gonna take a two-pronged answer,

01:11:21.600 --> 01:11:25.200
because there's actually a kind
of performance that evolved

01:11:25.200 --> 01:11:28.440
during lockdowns that wound
up being a lot more meaningful

01:11:28.440 --> 01:11:31.820
to me than I had expected,

01:11:31.820 --> 01:11:34.240
but it was not a live
performance situation.

01:11:34.240 --> 01:11:38.330
So, I prerecorded a
video of a world premier.

01:11:38.330 --> 01:11:40.710
And then it was a global world premier.

01:11:40.710 --> 01:11:43.620
It was Michael Abels'
piece, Isolation Variation.

01:11:43.620 --> 01:11:47.810
And it was commissioned as
part of a virtual residency

01:11:47.810 --> 01:11:49.530
at my request.

01:11:49.530 --> 01:11:54.530
And I got to watch during the premier,

01:11:55.550 --> 01:11:58.550
I got to watch who was
tuning in from where

01:11:58.550 --> 01:11:59.500
around the world.

01:11:59.500 --> 01:12:02.620
And usually world premieres
are just in a place

01:12:02.620 --> 01:12:03.800
whoever can get there.

01:12:03.800 --> 01:12:06.210
So, it's really just like a hall premier

01:12:06.210 --> 01:12:07.950
that's the very first one in the world,

01:12:07.950 --> 01:12:09.007
so it's a world premier.

01:12:09.007 --> 01:12:12.200
But it's not a global world
premier all at the same time.

01:12:12.200 --> 01:12:16.410
And so that, and then also
having that video then

01:12:16.410 --> 01:12:21.070
that I can send to other
virtual performances

01:12:21.070 --> 01:12:24.790
as a performance, it increases the reach

01:12:24.790 --> 01:12:26.230
of contemporary music.

01:12:26.230 --> 01:12:29.040
It increases the effectiveness
of one performance

01:12:29.040 --> 01:12:29.973
that I recorded.

01:12:31.210 --> 01:12:33.840
And so that's actually
been very special to me.

01:12:33.840 --> 01:12:34.900
That's the most special performance

01:12:34.900 --> 01:12:37.950
out of the lockdown experience.

01:12:37.950 --> 01:12:40.280
And I think we need to
keep those things going,

01:12:40.280 --> 01:12:43.480
because there's value in
that that we cannot achieve

01:12:43.480 --> 01:12:45.520
in a live performance in a hall.

01:12:45.520 --> 01:12:50.193
Obviously live performances
are not replicable virtually,

01:12:51.189 --> 01:12:53.920
and so we need to keep looking
at ways that are individual

01:12:53.920 --> 01:12:58.190
to live performances that
strengthen the strengths

01:12:58.190 --> 01:13:01.680
of live performance, and then
other formats that strengthen

01:13:01.680 --> 01:13:05.920
the strengths of virtual connections.

01:13:05.920 --> 01:13:08.103
For a live performance experience,

01:13:11.470 --> 01:13:15.190
I think personally the
most enlightening one was

01:13:15.190 --> 01:13:17.100
that Bach recital where
everything clicked,

01:13:17.100 --> 01:13:20.240
which happened to be at Lincoln Center.

01:13:20.240 --> 01:13:23.500
So I was returning to Lincoln
Center which I played in as

01:13:23.500 --> 01:13:26.610
which I played at as a teenager
and have played at since.

01:13:26.610 --> 01:13:31.243
I've gone to performances
there, but I had the entire...

01:13:31.243 --> 01:13:32.120
Oh, I don't know what
they call it anymore.

01:13:32.120 --> 01:13:33.817
It used to be Alice Tully
Hall, and they renamed it.

01:13:33.817 --> 01:13:35.590
And I was like an old person.

01:13:35.590 --> 01:13:37.763
So, I'm stuck back in Alice Tully times.

01:13:38.930 --> 01:13:43.340
But I was playing, and I was
the only person backstage.

01:13:43.340 --> 01:13:45.350
I was the only person on stage.

01:13:45.350 --> 01:13:48.090
I was the only person performing.

01:13:48.090 --> 01:13:51.380
And when I left the hall and
they turned out the lights

01:13:51.380 --> 01:13:54.800
for the hall, I was
like, "That was just me."

01:13:54.800 --> 01:13:58.340
And that was the concert
where I stayed in every note.

01:13:58.340 --> 01:14:02.740
And it was like this eternity
of a super special moment

01:14:02.740 --> 01:14:06.453
that just, it just drew
me through through it.

01:14:07.490 --> 01:14:08.937
And then the realization like,

01:14:08.937 --> 01:14:11.607
"Wow, look where I've landed." (laughs)

01:14:11.607 --> 01:14:13.800
I think no matter what we achieve in life,

01:14:13.800 --> 01:14:16.050
it's important to look at where we are.

01:14:16.050 --> 01:14:17.880
Where do we think we'd
be, and where are we now?

01:14:17.880 --> 01:14:19.803
And what are we proud of right now?

01:14:20.830 --> 01:14:23.080
In every class, in every
conversation with a student,

01:14:23.080 --> 01:14:24.580
I always ask, "What are you proud of?"

01:14:24.580 --> 01:14:27.500
Because I think we forget
to ask ourselves that.

01:14:27.500 --> 01:14:31.150
So, I think the moments
where I've been able

01:14:31.150 --> 01:14:33.847
to really sort of sit back and be like,

01:14:33.847 --> 01:14:37.500
"Whoa, this is a very cool
thing that music enables."

01:14:37.500 --> 01:14:39.130
Those are probably my
favorite performances.

01:14:39.130 --> 01:14:40.398
- [Student] Thank you.

01:14:40.398 --> 01:14:42.170
And I just wanted to mention,

01:14:42.170 --> 01:14:45.858
since you did bring up what
our audience means to you

01:14:45.858 --> 01:14:47.188
(indistinct)

01:14:47.188 --> 01:14:48.259
(audience laughing)

01:14:48.259 --> 01:14:50.236
(Hilary laughing)

01:14:50.236 --> 01:14:53.210
- Have a good day. (laughs)

01:14:53.210 --> 01:14:54.160
Oh, that's so cool.

01:14:56.200 --> 01:14:58.050
I'll let you select the...

01:14:58.050 --> 01:15:00.960
- Maybe the person in the Jean jacket.

01:15:00.960 --> 01:15:02.635
That's you, yeah.

01:15:02.635 --> 01:15:06.513
That is you, with red
shirt and Jean jacket, yes.

01:15:08.130 --> 01:15:09.953
- Do you need a mic, or person?

01:15:12.550 --> 01:15:15.345
You can say it and they
can translate it. (laughs)

01:15:15.345 --> 01:15:17.130
- [Andrew] I'm just gonna give it to her.

01:15:17.130 --> 01:15:18.570
- Okay.

01:15:18.570 --> 01:15:20.313
Living dangerously. Okay.

01:15:22.010 --> 01:15:24.360
- [Student] Thank you so
much for coming to Yale

01:15:24.360 --> 01:15:25.673
and giving your talk.

01:15:26.600 --> 01:15:28.130
It was really wonderful to hear you play.

01:15:28.130 --> 01:15:30.353
I'm a master student
at the School of Music.

01:15:31.650 --> 01:15:33.280
One of the things that
I'm very passionate about

01:15:33.280 --> 01:15:36.060
is the intersection of media and music.

01:15:36.060 --> 01:15:39.630
And one of your recent
projects was with David Lang,

01:15:39.630 --> 01:15:40.910
and involved AI.

01:15:40.910 --> 01:15:43.940
And I was curious about how
that came about and how you see

01:15:43.940 --> 01:15:48.940
the future of music interacting
with the new forms of media

01:15:50.170 --> 01:15:52.630
that is currently merging
and always changing

01:15:52.630 --> 01:15:55.773
and transforming our art form.

01:15:59.610 --> 01:16:04.610
- That was part of a
project that I co-founded.

01:16:04.660 --> 01:16:07.420
So, that was something
that I was working on a lot

01:16:07.420 --> 01:16:12.420
during part of my sabbatical
and also part of the season

01:16:12.880 --> 01:16:16.020
that mostly was canceled, last season.

01:16:16.020 --> 01:16:20.480
There's an AI scientist
named Carol Reiley who...

01:16:23.407 --> 01:16:25.870
She and I go way back socially.

01:16:25.870 --> 01:16:29.420
And we had been talking on the
phone about just a brainstorm

01:16:29.420 --> 01:16:32.410
of like, "Where does
music and AI overlap?"

01:16:32.410 --> 01:16:36.050
And I love talking with
people from different fields

01:16:36.050 --> 01:16:40.700
and kind of interpreting
their knowledge into a musical

01:16:40.700 --> 01:16:43.210
or artistic spectrum.

01:16:43.210 --> 01:16:44.935
Kind of like when I go
to a museum and I see

01:16:44.935 --> 01:16:49.230
the brush strokes of the
painting, or I see how an artist

01:16:49.230 --> 01:16:52.120
sort of dug into the paper with a pen

01:16:52.120 --> 01:16:55.620
and I can almost feel it
in my hand and translate it

01:16:55.620 --> 01:16:58.870
to a bow stroke, what would
it feel like to make that art?

01:16:58.870 --> 01:17:02.600
How can I make the sound
like that visual impression?

01:17:02.600 --> 01:17:07.200
And so I do that same parallel
drawing with other art forms,

01:17:07.200 --> 01:17:09.470
and I think AI...

01:17:09.470 --> 01:17:14.470
Interestingly, I think this,
but AI scientists do not,

01:17:14.500 --> 01:17:16.820
but I think AI is an art form,

01:17:16.820 --> 01:17:20.550
because there's a lot of
interpretation in coding.

01:17:20.550 --> 01:17:25.550
There's a lot of beauty in how AI programs

01:17:26.550 --> 01:17:29.150
and systems are structured.

01:17:29.150 --> 01:17:32.080
And AI is at a point right now with music,

01:17:32.080 --> 01:17:36.620
where a lot of the foundation
code is being written

01:17:36.620 --> 01:17:40.260
and a lot of the ethics
are being established.

01:17:40.260 --> 01:17:45.010
And so, it just seemed an
interesting thing to explore.

01:17:45.010 --> 01:17:49.880
Let's see where the state
of the art, arts (laughs)

01:17:49.880 --> 01:17:51.340
where the state of the art is.

01:17:51.340 --> 01:17:55.830
And let's see if these
composition programs work

01:17:55.830 --> 01:18:00.830
for composers on a really
high interpretive level,

01:18:00.970 --> 01:18:02.970
or is it more of a practical thing?

01:18:02.970 --> 01:18:05.920
And so it was sort of
testing the systems a bit,

01:18:05.920 --> 01:18:09.110
and it was tricky to find
composers who were interested

01:18:09.110 --> 01:18:13.270
in that, because in a way
when you're composing,

01:18:13.270 --> 01:18:17.650
it's hard enough without
having to learn a new language

01:18:17.650 --> 01:18:21.000
of composition, having to be restricted.

01:18:21.000 --> 01:18:22.620
But David has done a lot.

01:18:22.620 --> 01:18:25.150
A couple other composers
were interested as well.

01:18:25.150 --> 01:18:28.090
Michael Abels actually did
a piece that was premiered

01:18:28.090 --> 01:18:31.270
by Dominic Cheli, wonderful
pianist based in LA.

01:18:31.270 --> 01:18:35.060
And I was happy to see that
they kept working together.

01:18:35.060 --> 01:18:37.450
It makes me so happy when people,

01:18:37.450 --> 01:18:39.220
it's like artistic matchmaking.

01:18:39.220 --> 01:18:44.220
And I'm like, "Aw, you go do your thing."

01:18:44.740 --> 01:18:49.490
But that project, the idea
behind that project was to see

01:18:49.490 --> 01:18:51.990
if we could kind of bring
the fields together,

01:18:51.990 --> 01:18:55.210
'cause they kind of want to be together,

01:18:55.210 --> 01:18:57.973
but they kind of don't
know how to be together.

01:18:59.410 --> 01:19:04.410
A lot of people misunderstand
what AI is and what it can do,

01:19:05.480 --> 01:19:06.960
and what it can't do.

01:19:06.960 --> 01:19:10.990
And a lot of people misunderstand
what composition is,

01:19:10.990 --> 01:19:13.370
what it can do, what it can't do.

01:19:13.370 --> 01:19:16.749
But I have found it's
actually a lot harder to get

01:19:16.749 --> 01:19:19.300
those two fields talking to each other,

01:19:19.300 --> 01:19:22.693
because still the purposes are at odds.

01:19:23.580 --> 01:19:25.290
But there are places where they overlap.

01:19:25.290 --> 01:19:28.720
And so, I think that's the
direction we'll probably go

01:19:28.720 --> 01:19:32.290
with that project that we're developing.

01:19:32.290 --> 01:19:36.080
It's a little bit in suspense
because the original idea

01:19:36.080 --> 01:19:39.150
we had turned out, testing the systems,

01:19:39.150 --> 01:19:41.710
turned out to be quite complicated.

01:19:41.710 --> 01:19:44.120
But the pieces that came
out of it were really fun.

01:19:44.120 --> 01:19:49.120
So what David did was he
input a bunch of his work

01:19:49.490 --> 01:19:54.490
and then got the ideas
that the program gave him

01:19:57.690 --> 01:20:01.080
and made those into something.

01:20:01.080 --> 01:20:04.470
So he started the piece
that he wrote for me

01:20:04.470 --> 01:20:07.023
with the AIs ideas.

01:20:08.100 --> 01:20:11.300
He started it with his
melody and overlapped.

01:20:11.300 --> 01:20:15.130
So, he put an AI note in
between each of his notes.

01:20:15.130 --> 01:20:17.918
So at first, it's the
thing that he put in.

01:20:17.918 --> 01:20:20.830
And then he starts alternating.

01:20:20.830 --> 01:20:23.230
So it's not him collaborating with,

01:20:23.230 --> 01:20:26.030
it's them having a similar source,

01:20:26.030 --> 01:20:31.030
but kind of parallel making art.

01:20:31.300 --> 01:20:36.300
And then it ends with just the
notes that the AI was doing.

01:20:36.400 --> 01:20:38.340
So I think that's a really great idea,

01:20:38.340 --> 01:20:40.480
and that's something that is very David.

01:20:40.480 --> 01:20:44.080
It's very David Lang, and he's
a very open-minded composer.

01:20:44.080 --> 01:20:46.300
And in fact, he was one
taking it back full circle

01:20:46.300 --> 01:20:48.990
to what we were talking
about with the 27 Encores.

01:20:48.990 --> 01:20:53.990
He was one of the first
composers who was super on board

01:20:54.010 --> 01:20:57.490
with the idea of having everyone
together on that project.

01:20:57.490 --> 01:21:01.500
He's a very inclusive community builder

01:21:01.500 --> 01:21:06.500
among all of the composition
representatives these days.

01:21:08.810 --> 01:21:12.810
'Cause he does (indistinct)
and all these other projects.

01:21:12.810 --> 01:21:17.120
It was really nice to find
people who would be on board

01:21:17.120 --> 01:21:20.310
with this experiment, who
would struggle with it

01:21:20.310 --> 01:21:22.623
and make something really
interesting out of it.

01:21:23.479 --> 01:21:25.380
Does that answer the question?

01:21:25.380 --> 01:21:26.213
- [Student] Yeah.
- Okay.

01:21:26.213 --> 01:21:29.000
- [Student] And also, where is this going?

01:21:29.000 --> 01:21:29.833
- I dunno.

01:21:29.833 --> 01:21:31.348
(audience laughing)

01:21:31.348 --> 01:21:33.110
- That's where we currently are.

01:21:33.110 --> 01:21:36.400
I think people want projects
to have a clear goal,

01:21:36.400 --> 01:21:41.400
but sometimes the point is
exploring, where can the goal be?

01:21:44.850 --> 01:21:47.900
- So, I'm afraid we are
actually out of time.

01:21:47.900 --> 01:21:52.436
So, can you please all join
me in thanking Hilary Hahn.

01:21:52.436 --> 01:21:55.603
(audience applauding)

01:22:16.591 --> 01:22:20.258
(bright instrumental music)

